window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2015"] = {"id": "jammu-2015", "title": "University of Jammu 2015", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu M.A. Psychology entrance 2015, booklet code CNW-25353-B (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · 2015 · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Spearman said that there was a general ability employed by people while adjusting with different sorts of intellectual tasks, these factors are called the:", "options": {"a": "Specific factors", "b": "General factors", "c": "Multi factors", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Charles Spearman (1904) called the single ability that people use across every kind of intellectual task the general factor, or g. He noticed that schoolchildren who did well in one subject or test tended to do well in the others: all the scores correlated positively, a pattern later called the positive manifold. To explain it he developed an early form of factor analysis and proposed his two-factor theory. Every task draws on g, a common mental energy used in reasoning, comprehension and problem solving, plus a specific factor, s, that belongs to that one task alone (a special knack for arithmetic or for music). Because g is shared, it explains why different tests correlate; because each s is private to one test, it explains why those correlations are never perfect. For example, Arjun scores high in mathematics, vocabulary and puzzles alike; Spearman would credit his high g, while his extra flair for music would reflect a specific factor. Option b is right because the stem describes one ability employed while adjusting to different sorts of intellectual tasks, which is exactly the general factor. Exam trap: the stem says 'factors' in the plural, which pushes students towards 'specific factors' or 'multi factors'; but specific factors are tied to single tasks, and the multifactor theories were the ones that rejected a single dominant g.", "topic": "Spearman's general factor g", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true?", "options": {"a": "Creativity and intelligence are two related concepts", "b": "Creativity can never be linked with intelligence", "c": "(A) and (B) both are true", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: creativity and intelligence are two related concepts, linked but not the same. Intelligence, as IQ tests measure it, mostly rewards convergent thinking: finding the one correct answer. Creativity is producing ideas that are both novel and useful. J. P. Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, put divergent thinking at the heart of creativity: producing many varied answers, scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many categories), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how much detail). He placed both kinds of thinking inside one Structure of Intellect model, which already shows they belong together. Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) compared pupils who scored very high on creativity tests with pupils who scored very high on IQ and found the two groups overlapped only partly. The threshold hypothesis, associated with E. Paul Torrance, who built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking (1966), sums up the evidence: below an IQ of about 120 creativity and intelligence rise together, but above it they barely correlate. Overall the correlation is positive but modest. So Meera needs reasonable intelligence to invent a clever science project, but having a higher IQ than Kabir does not guarantee she is more creative. Option a is right because the two abilities are correlated and overlap without being identical. Exam trap: option c sounds generous, but 'related' and 'never linked' contradict each other, so both cannot be true at once.", "topic": "Creativity and intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Social Psychology gives emphasis on the individual's:", "options": {"a": "Behaviour", "b": "Phenomenology", "c": "Mind", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: social psychology gives emphasis to the individual's behaviour in social situations. Social psychology is the scientific study of how a person's behaviour, thought and feeling are shaped by other people. Gordon Allport (1897 to 1967) gave the classic definition: it tries to understand and explain how the thought, feeling and behaviour of individuals are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of others. Robert Baron and Donn Byrne, in their long-running textbook, call it the scientific field that seeks to understand the nature and causes of individual behaviour and thought in social situations, and older Indian textbooks define it simply as the scientific study of the behaviour of the individual in social situations. Two points follow. The unit of study is the individual (sociology studies the group or society as a whole), and the focus is behaviour that can be observed and measured. For example, Riya speaks freely with friends but stays silent in a large seminar; a social psychologist studies how the audience changes her behaviour. Option a is right because the standard definitions put the individual's behaviour in social settings at the centre. Exam trap: 'mind' and 'phenomenology' sound deeper, but they belong to other traditions, early structuralism and the humanistic or Gestalt description of lived experience, not the defining focus of social psychology. Key note: modern definitions add thought and feeling to behaviour, so 'mind' is not absurd; but the textbook definitions name the individual's behaviour as the core, so a stands.", "topic": "Definition of social psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Pick out the factor that does not play an important role in socialization of children.", "options": {"a": "Family and personal influence", "b": "Punishment and discipline", "c": "Social emotional development", "d": "Knowledge about arts and crafts"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: knowledge about arts and crafts does not play an important role in socialisation. Socialisation is the process by which a child learns the norms, values, roles, language and skills of the society, until they become the child's own and the child is accepted as a member. Its agents are the family (the primary agent), then school, peers, the media and religion. Its means include reward, punishment and discipline, which teach which behaviour is approved; imitation and modelling, shown in Albert Bandura's (1961) Bobo doll studies, where children copied an adult's aggression towards a doll; and identification with parents and other admired figures. Social-emotional development (attachment, self-control, empathy, reading others' feelings) is both a product of socialisation and a driver of it, because a child who understands others' feelings learns social rules faster. For example, little Kabir learns to share his toys because his mother praises him when he shares, scolds him when he snatches, and he copies his older sister. Option d is right because arts and crafts knowledge is one specific skill a child may or may not pick up; it does not shape how the child learns society's norms and roles. Exam trap: 'punishment and discipline' sounds negative, so students pick it as the odd one out, but discipline is one of the main means by which socialisation works.", "topic": "Factors in socialisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Group dynamic is a system of behaviour or psychological process occurring:", "options": {"a": "Within a social group", "b": "Between social groups", "c": "(A) and (B) both are true", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: group dynamics covers behaviour and psychological processes both within a social group and between social groups. Kurt Lewin (1890 to 1947), often called the father of modern social psychology, coined the term and in 1945 founded the Research Center for Group Dynamics at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. The standard definition calls group dynamics a system of behaviours and psychological processes occurring within a social group (intragroup dynamics) or between social groups (intergroup dynamics). Within a group it covers cohesion, roles, norms, leadership, conformity and decision making. Between groups it covers competition, cooperation, prejudice and conflict. Muzafer Sherif's Robbers Cave study (1954) showed the intergroup side clearly: boys at a summer camp, split into two groups, turned hostile through competition and came together again only through goals that needed both groups. For example, Arjun's college cricket team develops its own norms and chooses a captain (within the group), and then builds a fierce rivalry with the neighbouring college's team (between groups): both are group dynamics. Option c is right because the definition explicitly includes both levels. Exam trap: each single option is half of the definition, and option a looks complete because 'group dynamics' sounds as if it means one group, but intergroup processes are equally part of the field.", "topic": "Group dynamics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Communication is a process of meaningful interaction among human beings.\" This definition has been given by:", "options": {"a": "Allen", "b": "McFarland", "c": "Brown", "d": "Kelly"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the definition 'communication is a process of meaningful interaction among human beings' was given by Dalton E. McFarland, an American management writer, in his management textbook. He added that communication is the process by which meanings are perceived and understandings are reached among people. Two phrases carry the idea: 'process' (it unfolds over time, it is not a single act) and 'meaningful interaction' (both sides take part and share meaning). The basic elements of communication are a sender who encodes an idea, the message, a channel (speech, print, a phone screen), a receiver who decodes it, feedback that returns to the sender, and noise, anything that distorts the message on the way. Management texts often print McFarland's definition beside that of Louis A. Allen, who called communication the sum of all the things one person does when he wants to create understanding in the mind of another, 'a bridge of meaning'. For example, when Riya explains a new duty rota to her team and they ask questions until everyone understands it, that two-way exchange of meaning is communication in McFarland's sense. Option b is right because the 'meaningful interaction among human beings' wording is McFarland's. Exam trap: Allen's definition is often quoted next to it, so students mix up the 'bridge of meaning' line with the 'meaningful interaction' line.", "topic": "Definitions of communication", "fig": "Dalton E. McFarland"}, {"q": "Characteristics of the source which have been shown to increase attitude change are:", "options": {"a": "Credibility and attractiveness", "b": "High credibility and high attractiveness", "c": "High attractiveness and low attractiveness", "d": "High credibility and low credibility"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: high credibility and high attractiveness are the source characteristics shown to increase attitude change. In the 1950s Carl Hovland led the Yale Communication Research Program; his book with Irving Janis and Harold Kelley, Communication and Persuasion (1953), broke persuasion into who says what to whom: the source, the message and the audience. Two source factors stand out. Credibility combines expertise (the source knows the facts) and trustworthiness (the source has no reason to mislead). Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss (1951) gave students the same article on atomic submarines, credited either to the physicist Robert Oppenheimer or to the Soviet newspaper Pravda; the high-credibility version changed more opinions, although four weeks later the gap had almost vanished, the sleeper effect. Attractiveness covers likeability, physical attractiveness and similarity to the audience; Shelly Chaiken (1979) found that physically attractive student communicators persuaded more people to sign a petition. For example, Meera is more likely to switch toothpaste on the advice of a dentist she likes than on the word of an anonymous stranger. Option b is right because it is a high level of each trait, not merely its presence, that raises persuasion. Exam trap: options c and d pair high with low on the same trait, which describes no real source and is meaningless. Key note: option a names the same two traits; what actually increases attitude change is a high level of each, so b is the more exact answer and stands. If a source lists a, it is the same idea.", "topic": "Source credibility and attractiveness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Common features of prejudice do not include:", "options": {"a": "Negative feelings", "b": "Stereotyped behaviours", "c": "Rational and flexible opinions", "d": "Prejudgement about members of a group"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: rational and flexible opinions are not a feature of prejudice; they are its opposite. Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, defined ethnic prejudice as an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalisation, which may be felt or expressed, towards a whole group or towards an individual because he is a member of that group. Two words carry the meaning: faulty (it is not based on accurate evidence) and inflexible (it resists contrary evidence). Prejudice is usually described with three components, the ABC model: affective (negative feelings such as dislike, fear or contempt), cognitive (stereotypes and prejudgement of group members before knowing them as individuals) and behavioural (discrimination and stereotyped actions towards them). For example, Arjun refuses to rent his flat to students from another state (behaviour) because he believes they are all noisy (stereotype) and feels irritated at the very thought (feeling); when a quiet, tidy student from that state applies, he dismisses her as an exception, which shows how rigid prejudice is. Option c is right because prejudice is irrational and rigid, the reverse of rational and flexible opinion. Exam trap: the stem's 'do not include' is easy to miss; the other three options each describe one genuine component of prejudice.", "topic": "Components of prejudice", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is true for interpersonal attraction?", "options": {"a": "Only personal characteristics play a role in interpersonal attraction", "b": "Only the environment plays a role in interpersonal attraction", "c": "Both personality characteristics and environment play a role in interpersonal attraction", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: both personality characteristics and the environment play a role in interpersonal attraction. Interpersonal attraction is a positive attitude towards another person, the wish to be near them and to like them. Its causes are usually sorted into two kinds. Personal factors lie in the people themselves: physical attractiveness (in Elaine Walster and colleagues' 1966 computer dance study, looks best predicted whether students wanted another date), similarity of attitudes and values (Donn Byrne, The Attraction Paradigm, 1971), and reciprocity, liking those who like us. Situational or environmental factors lie in the setting: proximity, shown by Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) in Westgate West, married-student housing at MIT, where next-door neighbours were named as close friends far more often than people further down the corridor; mere exposure, Robert Zajonc's (1968) finding that repeated contact alone increases liking; and arousal, shown in Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron's (1974) suspension bridge study, where men on a frightening bridge were more attracted to a female interviewer. For example, Riya and Kabir become close friends because they sit next to each other in class (proximity) and discover they share a love of cricket (similarity). Option c is right because attraction depends on the people and the situation together. Exam trap: the word 'only' in options a and b is the giveaway; each names one real set of causes but excludes the other.", "topic": "Determinants of interpersonal attraction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following may cause not only lasting physical injury, but reduce efficiency by causing mental fatigue and loss of mental alertness?", "options": {"a": "Pollution", "b": "Noise", "c": "Yoga", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: noise can cause lasting physical injury and also reduce efficiency through mental fatigue and loss of alertness. Noise is unwanted sound, and it is the classic working-conditions factor studied in industrial and environmental psychology, alongside illumination, temperature, ventilation and music at work. Its physical effect is direct: long exposure to loud sound, roughly above 85 decibels across a working day, damages the hair cells of the inner ear and causes permanent noise-induced hearing loss. Its psychological effects are subtler: noise raises stress, fatigue and irritability, breaks concentration and increases errors. David Glass and Jerome Singer (1972) showed that unpredictable, uncontrollable bursts of noise lowered people's later frustration tolerance and proofreading accuracy, even after the noise had stopped; when people believed they could switch the noise off, these after-effects shrank. For example, Arjun, who works a full shift beside a loud printing press, slowly loses some of his hearing and by the afternoon makes more mistakes and feels drained. Option b is right because the stem pairs lasting physical injury with mental fatigue and loss of alertness, the standard textbook description of the effects of noise. Exam trap: pollution also harms health, so it is tempting, but the textbook passage on mental fatigue and lost alertness at work is about noise.", "topic": "Effects of noise", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to whom, the personality is \"the distinctive patterns of behaviour that characterize each individual's adaptation to the situation of his or her life\"?", "options": {"a": "Gordon Allport", "b": "Walter Mischel", "c": "Allport and Odbert", "d": "Potkay and Allen"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Walter Mischel defined personality as 'the distinctive patterns of behaviour (including thoughts and emotions) that characterise each individual's adaptation to the situations of his or her life'. Walter Mischel (1930 to 2018) is best known for Personality and Assessment (1968), which argued that trait scores predict behaviour in any one situation only weakly and so started the person-situation debate. His definition, from his textbook Introduction to Personality, fits that view: it stresses patterns of behaviour and adaptation to situations rather than fixed inner traits. Compare Gordon Allport (1937), whose famous definition calls personality 'the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment'. Allport's wording points inside the person (organisation, psychophysical systems); Mischel's points to observable patterns across life situations. Mischel later described these patterns as stable if-then profiles: if the situation is X, the person does A; if it is Y, the person does B. For example, Meera is talkative with friends but quiet with elders, and reliably so; for Mischel that stable pattern of adapting to situations is her personality. Option b is right because the quoted words, 'distinctive patterns of behaviour' and 'adaptation to the situations of his or her life', are Mischel's. Exam trap: Allport's definition also mentions adjustment to the environment, so students confuse the two; look for 'dynamic organisation' (Allport) versus 'distinctive patterns of behaviour' (Mischel).", "topic": "Mischel's definition of personality", "fig": "Walter Mischel"}, {"q": "The view of Freud \"males are dominant or superior to females\" is rejected by:", "options": {"a": "Alfred Adler", "b": "Anna Freud", "c": "Karen Horney", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Karen Horney rejected Freud's view that males are naturally superior to females. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) held that a girl discovers she lacks a penis, feels penis envy and inferiority, and develops a weaker superego than a boy; for him, female inferiority was rooted in anatomy. Karen Horney (1885 to 1952), a German psychoanalyst who later worked in the USA, challenged this from 1922 onwards. She argued that the idea of penis envy was drawn from neurotic patients and seen through male eyes, and she proposed that men may feel womb envy, envy of women's power to bear children, which they sublimate into achievement. Women's feelings of inferiority, she said, come from their social and cultural subordination, such as economic dependence and narrow roles, not from biology. Her essays were later collected as Feminine Psychology (1967). For example, if Riya feels less capable than her brother, Horney would look at a family that gave him all the opportunities, not at her body. Option c is right because Horney is the theorist textbooks credit with directly rejecting Freud's idea of female inferiority. Exam trap: Anna Freud sounds plausible because she was a famous woman analyst, but she extended her father's theory loyally instead of attacking it. Key note: Alfred Adler also attacked the cultural overvaluation of men (his 'masculine protest'), so a is not absurd; but Horney is the theorist textbooks credit with directly rejecting Freud's view of female inferiority, so c stands.", "topic": "Horney's feminine psychology", "fig": "Karen Horney"}, {"q": "In Adler's view, the main driving force in personality is:", "options": {"a": "Striving for superiority", "b": "Striving for inferiority", "c": "Collective unconscious", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: for Alfred Adler the main driving force in personality is the striving for superiority. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), an early colleague of Sigmund Freud who broke away in 1911, founded individual psychology. He began with organ inferiority (his 1907 study argued that people compensate for a weak organ or body part) and then widened the idea: every child starts small and dependent, so everyone has feelings of inferiority. These feelings are not a weakness but the spur to growth. We strive to overcome them, and this striving for superiority, later called the striving for perfection or completion, is the master motive. Healthy striving is guided by social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), concern for others and the community; unhealthy striving turns into a selfish superiority complex. Each person's unique way of striving is the style of life, aimed at a fictional final goal (fictional finalism). For example, Kabir stammered as a child and worked hard to become a fine debater; Adler would see compensation for felt inferiority driving his striving. Option a is right because Adler named the striving for superiority as the one dynamic force behind behaviour. Exam trap: 'striving for inferiority' borrows Adler's own vocabulary, but inferiority feelings are what we strive away from, not a goal anyone pursues.", "topic": "Adler's striving for superiority", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "Carl Jung noted that between the ego and the outside world we often find a:", "options": {"a": "Basic anxiety", "b": "Style of life", "c": "Feeling of superiority", "d": "Persona"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Carl Jung placed the persona between the ego and the outside world. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), founder of analytical psychology, took the word persona from the Latin for the mask an actor wore on stage. The persona is the social face or role we show to others, shaped by what society expects of us: the polite student, the confident doctor, the dutiful son. It mediates between the ego, the centre of conscious awareness, and the outer world, helping us adapt and get along. Jung counted it among the archetypes, the universal patterns of the collective unconscious, alongside the shadow (the disowned dark side), the anima and animus (the feminine side in a man and the masculine side in a woman) and the self (the archetype of wholeness). The persona is useful, but Jung warned against identifying too closely with it, so that the person loses touch with the real self behind the mask. For example, Arjun is always cheerful and obliging at the office; if he comes to believe that role is all he is, he has become over-identified with his persona. Option d is right because the persona is exactly the mask that stands between the ego and the outside world. Exam trap: the other three options are real neo-Freudian terms, but they belong to Karen Horney and Alfred Adler, not to Jung.", "topic": "Jung's persona", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Which of the following include phobia, panic disorder, obsessive compulsive disorder, and post-traumatic stress disorder?", "options": {"a": "Anxiety disorders", "b": "Conduct disorders", "c": "Oppositional Defiant disorder", "d": "Mood disorder"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: phobia, panic disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) were grouped together as anxiety disorders. In DSM-IV-TR (2000) the anxiety disorders chapter included specific phobia, social phobia, agoraphobia, panic disorder, OCD, PTSD, acute stress disorder and generalised anxiety disorder (GAD). What united them was anxiety as the core feature: excessive fear of a particular object or situation in phobias; sudden attacks of intense fear in panic disorder; anxiety driven by intrusive thoughts and relieved by rituals in OCD; and fear and hyperarousal after a trauma in PTSD. DSM-5 (2013) reorganised the group, moving OCD into a new chapter, Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders, and PTSD into Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders, while the phobias and panic disorder stayed in the anxiety chapter. For example, Meera avoids lifts (phobia), Arjun has sudden pounding-heart attacks (panic), Kabir checks the gas knob twenty times (OCD) and Riya relives an accident in nightmares (PTSD): older texts file all four as anxiety disorders. Option a is right because anxiety is the shared core of all four conditions listed. Exam trap: the other options are real diagnostic categories, so they look plausible, but none of them contains phobias or panic. Key note: since DSM-5, of the four listed, only the phobias and panic disorder are still anxiety disorders; the item uses the older DSM-IV grouping (all four share anxiety as the core feature), and of these options anxiety disorders stands.", "topic": "Grouping of anxiety disorders", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is the current version of Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of mental disorders?", "options": {"a": "DSM-V", "b": "DSM-IV", "c": "DSM-IV-TR", "d": "DSM-III"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: DSM-V, properly written DSM-5, was the current edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders in 2015. The manual is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Its editions run DSM-I (1952), DSM-II (1968), DSM-III (1980), DSM-III-R (1987), DSM-IV (1994), DSM-IV-TR (2000, a text revision) and DSM-5 (May 2013). DSM-III was the turning point: it brought explicit diagnostic criteria, a descriptive stance that is neutral about causes, and the multiaxial system of five axes. DSM-5 switched from Roman to Arabic numerals so that later updates could be numbered, dropped the multiaxial system, regrouped disorders into new chapters (OCD and the trauma disorders left the anxiety chapter) and added dimensional ratings. For example, when Kabir, a trainee clinician in 2015, diagnosed a child with autism spectrum disorder, he used DSM-5, in which older separate labels such as Asperger's disorder had been merged into one spectrum. Option a is right because DSM-5 is the newest edition among the options and was the edition in use in 2015. Exam trap: printing 'DSM-V' in Roman numerals tempts students to think it is a fake option, but it simply means DSM-5. Key note: the current edition today is DSM-5-TR (March 2022); when this paper was set DSM-5 was current, and it is still the newest edition among these options, so a stands.", "topic": "DSM editions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of phobia?", "options": {"a": "Social phobia", "b": "Specific phobia (or simple phobia)", "c": "Obsessive compulsive disorder", "d": "Agoraphobia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is not a type of phobia. A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object, situation or activity, out of proportion to the real danger, which the person avoids or endures with great distress. DSM-IV and ICD-10 recognise three types. Specific (simple) phobia is fear of one particular object or situation; DSM-5 lists five subtypes: animal (dogs, snakes), natural environment (heights, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying) and other. Social phobia, now also called social anxiety disorder, is fear of being watched, judged or embarrassed in social or performance situations. Agoraphobia is fear of places where escape might be difficult or help unavailable, such as crowds, public transport or being outside the home alone. OCD is a separate disorder: obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts or images (contamination, harm), and compulsions are repetitive acts or mental rituals (washing, checking, counting) performed to reduce the anxiety the obsessions cause. For example, Riya's terror of dogs is a specific phobia, while Kabir's hour of handwashing after touching a door handle, driven by intrusive thoughts of germs, is OCD. Option c is right because OCD is defined by obsessions and compulsions, not by fear of an object or situation. Exam trap: OCD involves anxiety and, until DSM-5, sat in the same anxiety chapter as the phobias, but sharing a chapter does not make it a phobia.", "topic": "Types of phobia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The person with generalised anxiety disorder:", "options": {"a": "Worries about everyday routine events", "b": "Does not worry about everyday routine events", "c": "Has no routine everyday events to worry about", "d": "Is carefree and settled in mood"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: a person with generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) worries about everyday routine events. DSM-5 defines GAD by excessive anxiety and worry about a number of events or activities, such as work, school, health, money and ordinary daily matters, occurring more days than not for at least 6 months. The person finds the worry hard to control, and it comes with at least 3 of 6 symptoms in adults (only 1 in children): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily fatigued, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension and sleep disturbance. Because the worry is not tied to one object, older texts called it free-floating anxiety. Thomas Borkovec, from the early 1990s, proposed that worry works as cognitive avoidance: thinking about threats in words dampens the vivid, frightening images and bodily arousal of fear, so the worry is rewarded and keeps going. For example, Meera lies awake worrying whether her son reached school safely, whether the electricity bill was paid and whether a mild headache means something serious, day after day for months; that pattern is GAD. Option a is right because the hallmark of GAD is persistent, hard-to-control worry about ordinary, everyday matters. Exam trap: students sometimes assume an anxiety disorder needs a dramatic trigger, but in GAD the worry spreads over routine life, unlike the single feared object of a phobia or the sudden attacks of panic disorder.", "topic": "Generalised anxiety disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The first text book of experimental psychology \"Principles of Physiological Psychology\" was published by Wilhelm Wundt in:", "options": {"a": "1874", "b": "1920", "c": "1958", "d": "1961"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Wilhelm Wundt published Principles of Physiological Psychology (Grundzüge der physiologischen Psychologie) in 1874. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician who had assisted Hermann von Helmholtz, brought the book out in two parts, in 1873 and 1874, and the date is usually given as 1874, when it was complete. It is often called the first textbook of experimental psychology. In it Wundt argued that psychology should be an independent science that studies conscious experience with the experimental methods of physiology; 'physiological' meant borrowing experimental method, not reducing the mind to the body. Five years later, in 1879, his students began research in the Leipzig laboratory, the conventional birth of scientific psychology, and in 1881 he founded the journal Philosophische Studien. Students who trained with him, such as Edward Titchener and James McKeen Cattell, carried the new science to the USA. A simple timeline helps Arjun remember: book 1874, laboratory 1879, journal 1881. Option a is right because 1874 is the year the book appeared in full. Exam trap: 1920 is a real Wundt date, but it is the year he died, not the year of the book.", "topic": "Wundt's first textbook", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "Other name of control variable is:", "options": {"a": "Manipulated variable", "b": "Quantitative variable", "c": "Relevant variable", "d": "Extraneous variable"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a control variable is an extraneous variable that the experimenter holds constant. In an experiment the independent variable (IV) is what the experimenter manipulates, and the dependent variable (DV) is the outcome measured. An extraneous variable is any other variable that might affect the DV: participant variables such as age, intelligence and fatigue; task variables such as instructions; and environmental or situational variables such as noise, lighting and time of day. When the experimenter holds such a variable constant or otherwise neutralises it, it becomes a control variable; if it is left uncontrolled and changes along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable and spoils the causal conclusion. The techniques of control are elimination, holding conditions constant, matching, counterbalancing and random assignment. For example, Riya tests whether background music (IV) affects memory scores (DV); she runs every participant at 10 am in the same quiet room, so time of day and noise, two extraneous variables, are now control variables. Option d is right because control variables are extraneous variables brought under control, which is why the two terms are paired. Exam trap: 'manipulated variable' sounds like something the experimenter controls, but it is the other name for the IV, the one variable deliberately varied rather than held constant. Key note: some Indian texts call the extraneous variables that can affect the DV 'relevant variables', so c is close; but the standard pairing in research methods texts is that a control variable is an extraneous variable held constant, so d stands.", "topic": "Control and extraneous variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The key features of experimental psychology do not include:", "options": {"a": "Control over variables", "b": "Introspection", "c": "Careful measurement", "d": "Establishing cause and effect relationship"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: introspection is not a key feature of experimental psychology as the method is understood today. An experiment is a controlled test of a cause-and-effect idea. The researcher manipulates one variable (the independent variable), holds other conditions constant or balances them by random assignment (control over variables), and records the outcome with careful, objective and repeatable measurement. Because only the manipulated variable differs between conditions, a change in the outcome can be traced to it, so the experiment is the one method that can establish cause and effect. Example: Arjun randomly assigns 40 students to study with or without background music, keeps the room, time and word list the same, and counts the words recalled; if the music group recalls fewer, he can say the music caused the drop. Introspection, by contrast, is trained self-observation of one's own conscious experience. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) used a controlled form of it in his Leipzig laboratory, and Edward Titchener (1898) made it the core of structuralism. It fell out of favour because private reports could not be checked or agreed (the Würzburg school's imageless-thought reports clashed with Titchener's), and John B. Watson (1913) rejected it altogether. Option b is right because introspection is a subjective report of experience, not a feature of controlled, objective experimentation. Exam trap: because Wundt founded experimental psychology and used introspection, students assume introspection belongs to the experimental method; the features examiners list are control, manipulation, measurement and causal inference. Key note: Wundt's own laboratory did use controlled introspection, so the option is not absurd historically; but it is not a feature of experimental psychology as the method is taught today, so b stands.", "topic": "Features of the experimental method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In experimental method the variable which is manipulated, is called:", "options": {"a": "Independent variable", "b": "Dependent variable", "c": "Extraneous variable", "d": "Control variable"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the variable that is manipulated in an experiment is called the independent variable. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes one factor, sets its levels and decides which group gets which level; this is the independent variable (IV), the presumed cause. The dependent variable (DV) is the outcome that is measured to see whether it changes with the IV, the presumed effect; its value 'depends on' the IV. Extraneous variables are all the other factors that could also affect the DV, such as time of day, noise or fatigue. An extraneous variable that the researcher holds constant becomes a control variable; one that is left free and varies along with the IV becomes a confounding variable, which spoils the causal conclusion. Robert Woodworth's textbook Experimental Psychology (1938) did much to make the IV and DV terms standard in psychology. Example: Kabir gives participants 0, 100 or 200 mg of caffeine (IV) and measures their reaction time in milliseconds (DV), testing everyone at 10 a.m. so that time of day is a control variable. Option a is right because the IV is defined as the variable the experimenter manipulates or varies, independently of anything the participant does. Exam trap: students swap IV and DV; remember that you manipulate the IV and measure the DV, and that a control variable is held constant, not manipulated.", "topic": "Independent variable", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Our first contact with the physical world is through:", "options": {"a": "Sensations", "b": "Sense organs", "c": "Attentional process", "d": "Photoreceptors"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: our first contact with the physical world is through sensations. Sensation is the process by which sensory receptors detect physical energy (light, sound waves, pressure, heat, chemicals) and convert it into neural signals, a step called transduction. The result is the most basic, elementary experience: a patch of red, a tone, warmth on the skin, a bitter taste. Perception comes next: the brain organises and interprets these sensations into meaningful objects and events (a red apple, a friend's voice). Sense organs such as the eye, ear and skin are the channels or gateways through which energy reaches us, and attention then selects which sensations receive further processing. Example: when Meera steps into a dark room and sees a faint glow, the raw experience of light is the sensation; recognising it as a mobile phone screen is perception. Option a is right because sensation is the first psychological event in the chain, the elementary experience on which all perception and knowledge of the world are built. Exam trap: examiners offer 'sense organs' because books say we know the world 'through our senses'; but an organ is a bodily structure, while the contact itself, as experienced, is the sensation. Key note: some books say we know the world through our sense organs, so b is close; but the contact itself is the sensation (sense organs are the gateway it comes through), so a stands.", "topic": "Sensation as first contact", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is the monocular cue for depth perception?", "options": {"a": "Linear perspective", "b": "Interposition", "c": "Shadows", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: linear perspective, interposition and shadows are all monocular cues for depth perception. Depth cues are pieces of information the brain uses to judge distance in a three-dimensional world from flat retinal images. Binocular cues need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye gets a slightly different view, and the difference is larger for near objects) and convergence (the eyes turn inwards for near objects). Monocular cues work with one eye, and most are pictorial cues that painters use. Linear perspective: parallel lines, such as railway tracks, seem to converge as they recede. Interposition (overlap): a nearer object hides part of a farther one, so the hidden object is seen as farther. Light and shadow (shading): the way light falls and shadows are cast shows depth and whether a surface bulges or dips. Other monocular cues are relative size, texture gradient (James Gibson, 1950), aerial perspective (haze), height in the visual field, motion parallax and accommodation. Example: Kabir closes one eye while looking down his college corridor; the converging floor lines, the door half hidden by a pillar and the shadows under the benches still tell him what is near and what is far. Option d is right because each of the three listed cues works with a single eye. Exam trap: retinal disparity and convergence are the only binocular cues; examiners often slip one of them into a list of monocular cues, or offer motion parallax, which is monocular even though it needs movement.", "topic": "Monocular depth cues", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of reaction time?", "options": {"a": "Simple reaction time", "b": "Choice reaction time", "c": "Discrimination reaction time", "d": "Control reaction time"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'control reaction time' is not a type of reaction time. Reaction time (RT) is the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the start of a response. Franciscus Donders (1868), a Dutch physiologist, described the three classic types. Simple RT (his a-reaction): one stimulus, one response, as when Riya presses a key the moment any light appears. Choice RT (b-reaction): several stimuli, each with its own response, as when Riya presses the left key for red and the right key for green. Discrimination RT (c-reaction, now called go/no-go): several stimuli, but only one calls for a response, as when Riya presses only for red and holds back for green. Donders' subtraction method treated the extra time as the duration of the extra mental stage: discrimination RT minus simple RT estimates the time needed to identify the stimulus, and choice RT minus discrimination RT estimates the time needed to select a response. This began mental chronometry. Simple RT is the shortest and choice RT the longest, and William Hick (1952) showed that choice RT rises with the logarithm of the number of alternatives (Hick's law). Option d is right because no standard classification lists a 'control' reaction time; the word is borrowed from experimental control to sound plausible. Exam trap: discrimination RT is real (some books call it recognition RT), so students strike it out by mistake; it is Donders' c-reaction.", "topic": "Types of reaction time", "fig": "Franciscus Donders"}, {"q": "Attention is:", "options": {"a": "A behaviour as well as cognitive process", "b": "Only behavioural process", "c": "Only a mental process", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: attention is a behaviour as well as a cognitive process. Attention is the selection of some stimuli for further processing while others are ignored. William James (1890) described it as 'the taking possession by the mind, in clear and vivid form, of one out of what seem several simultaneously possible objects or trains of thought'. That is its cognitive side: focusing, selecting and holding information in awareness. But attention also has an observable side. When a sudden sound occurs, people and animals turn the head and eyes towards it, pause what they are doing and adjust their posture; Ivan Pavlov (1927) called this the 'what is it?' reflex, now known as the orienting reflex. Older texts also describe a preparatory set, the bodily readiness to receive an expected stimulus. Example: when Kabir hears his name called in a noisy canteen, he stops chewing, turns his head and fixes his eyes on the speaker (behaviour) while his mind picks out that one voice from the chatter (cognition). Option a is right because attention involves both the mental act of selection and the overt orienting and adjusting behaviour that goes with it. Exam trap: modern books define attention as a cognitive process, so students pick 'only a mental process'; the word 'only' is the trap, since attention also shows itself in behaviour. Key note: many modern textbooks simply call attention a cognitive process, so c is close; but the item counts the overt orienting behaviour that comes with it, and attention involves both, so a stands.", "topic": "Nature of attention", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following statistics provides a shorthand summary of large number of observations?", "options": {"a": "Pure", "b": "Descriptive", "c": "Numerical", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: descriptive statistics provide a shorthand summary of a large number of observations. Statistics in psychology has two broad branches. Descriptive statistics organise, summarise and describe the data actually collected: frequency tables and graphs, measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode), measures of variability (range, quartile deviation, standard deviation), the shape of the distribution (skewness, kurtosis) and correlation. Each turns hundreds of raw scores into a few numbers or a single picture. Inferential statistics go further: they use probability to generalise from a sample to the population it came from, with tools such as the t test, the F test (ANOVA), chi-square and confidence intervals. Example: Meera has anxiety scores from 200 students in Mysuru. Saying 'the mean is 42, the SD is 8 and the scores are slightly positively skewed' is descriptive; testing whether boys and girls in the whole state differ is inferential. Option b is right because summarising many observations in a few indices is exactly what descriptive statistics are defined to do. Exam trap: the usual rival is 'inferential statistics', which generalise rather than summarise; invented labels such as 'pure' or 'numerical' statistics are not branches at all, and the cue word 'summary' always points to descriptive statistics.", "topic": "Descriptive statistics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A frequency distribution when represented by means of a graph makes the unwieldy data:", "options": {"a": "Intelligible", "b": "Correct", "c": "Wrong", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: representing a frequency distribution by a graph makes unwieldy data intelligible. A frequency distribution arranges raw scores into class intervals and shows how many cases fall in each. Even as a table it can be hard to take in, so it is often drawn as a graph. The common forms are the histogram (adjoining bars whose heights show frequencies), the frequency polygon (a line joining the midpoints of the intervals), the cumulative frequency curve or ogive (used to read medians and percentiles), and, for categories, the bar diagram and pie chart. Classic texts such as Henry Garrett's Statistics in Psychology and Education (1926), long standard in Indian classrooms, list the uses: a graph shows the shape of the distribution at a glance (symmetry, skew, peaks, gaps), allows quick comparison of two groups, attracts attention and is easy to remember. Example: Arjun has the marks of 300 students in a long list; drawn as a histogram, it shows at once that most students scored 50 to 60 and that a small cluster failed. Option a is right because the purpose of a graph is to make a mass of figures understandable without changing them. Exam trap: a graph only displays data; it cannot correct errors, so options about making data 'correct' play on the false idea that presentation improves accuracy.", "topic": "Graphs of frequency distributions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Range is not a reliable measure of:", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "Dispersion", "c": "Median", "d": "Mode"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: range is not a reliable measure of dispersion. Dispersion (variability) describes how spread out scores are around the centre. The range is the simplest measure: the highest score minus the lowest score. It is quick to compute and easy to understand, but it is unreliable for three reasons. It depends on only two scores, the two extremes, and ignores everything in between. A single outlier can change it drastically. It also tends to grow as the sample grows, because a larger sample is more likely to include an extreme case. Example: in Riya's class, 39 students score between 40 and 75, a range of 35; one more student who scores 98 pushes the range to 58, although none of the other 39 marks has moved. More stable measures of dispersion are the quartile deviation (semi-interquartile range, half the distance between the first and third quartiles), the average deviation and, best of all, the standard deviation, which uses every score. Option b is right because range is classed as a measure of dispersion, and its dependence on two extreme scores makes it the least reliable one. Exam trap: mean, median and mode are measures of central tendency; the range does not measure them at all, so these options mix up the two families of descriptive statistics.", "topic": "Range as a measure of dispersion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An ideal measure of which of the following should be rigidly defined and its values should be definite?", "options": {"a": "Central tendency", "b": "Frequency distribution", "c": "Median", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: an ideal measure of central tendency should be rigidly defined, so that its value is definite. A measure of central tendency is a single value that represents the centre of a set of scores: the mean, median or mode. The statistician George Udny Yule (1911), in An Introduction to the Theory of Statistics, listed the requisites of a satisfactory average: it should be rigidly defined (everyone computing it gets the same definite value, not one left to judgement), easy to understand, easy to calculate, based on all the observations, capable of further algebraic treatment and as little affected as possible by fluctuations of sampling. Indian texts add that it should not be unduly affected by extreme values. The arithmetic mean meets almost all of these, which is why it is the most used average; its weakness is that extreme scores pull it. The median resists extremes but ignores the size of most scores, and the mode can be ill defined when a distribution has two peaks. Example: if Kabir and Meera both calculate the mean of the same 50 scores, they must get the same answer; a rough mode read from a graph might differ between them. Option a is right because these requisites are the classic test of an ideal average, that is, of a measure of central tendency. Exam trap: the median is one particular measure, not the category the requisites describe.", "topic": "Ideal measure of central tendency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In random sampling method:", "options": {"a": "Each individual in the population has an equal likelihood of selection", "b": "Population size is not known to the researcher", "c": "(A) and (B) both", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: in random sampling each individual in the population has an equal likelihood of selection. Simple random sampling is the basic probability sampling method. Every member of the population has an equal chance of being chosen, and each choice is independent of the others, so no person or type of person is favoured. To do this the researcher needs a sampling frame, a complete list of the population, and then draws names by lottery, from a table of random numbers (the first published table was Leonard Tippett's, 1927) or with a computer random number generator. Because chance alone decides who is chosen, the sample tends to represent the population, and the laws of probability can be used to estimate sampling error. Example: Arjun wants 100 of the 2,000 students in his college; he numbers the full enrolment list from 1 to 2,000 and lets a computer pick 100 numbers at random. Option a is right because equal probability of selection is the defining feature of random sampling. Exam trap: students think 'random' means haphazard or unplanned; in fact random sampling needs the population to be known and listed, which makes option b false, not true.", "topic": "Simple random sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Purposive sampling is a type of:", "options": {"a": "Non-probability sampling", "b": "Probability sampling", "c": "(A) and (B) both", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: purposive sampling is a type of non-probability sampling. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection, decided by chance: simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability sampling selection depends on the researcher's choice or on convenience, so the chance of any person being picked is unknown: convenience (accidental), purposive, quota and snowball sampling. Purposive (judgemental) sampling means the researcher deliberately picks cases judged to be typical or rich in information for the purpose of the study. Example: Meera, studying burnout in therapists, deliberately chooses 10 senior clinical psychologists in Delhi because they have the experience she needs for in-depth interviews. Purposive sampling is quick, cheap and well suited to expert interviews and qualitative work, but its results cannot be generalised with statistical confidence and it is open to the researcher's bias. Option a is right because inclusion depends on the researcher's judgement, not on chance, which places purposive sampling in the non-probability family. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into groups, but quota fills the groups by convenience, so it too is non-probability.", "topic": "Purposive sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the main property of normal distribution?", "options": {"a": "The normal distribution is defined by two parameters: mean and standard deviation", "b": "Sixty-eight percent of all its observations fall within plus or minus 1 standard deviation", "c": "A range of plus or minus 1.96 standard deviations covers 95% of the scores", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements are properties of the normal distribution. The normal distribution (normal probability curve) is a bell-shaped, symmetrical, unimodal curve in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and whose tails approach the baseline without ever touching it. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it to describe errors of measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. Statement A is true: the curve is fully defined by two parameters, the mean (which fixes its centre) and the standard deviation (which fixes its spread); know these two and every area under the curve follows. Statement B is true: about 68 per cent (68.26) of cases lie within 1 SD either side of the mean; about 95.44 per cent lie within 2 SD and 99.73 per cent within 3 SD. Statement C is true: exactly 95 per cent lie within plus or minus 1.96 SD, which is why 1.96 is used for 95 per cent confidence intervals; plus or minus 2.58 SD covers 99 per cent. Example: IQ scores have a mean of 100 and an SD of 15, so about 68 per cent of people, Riya among them, score between 85 and 115. Option d is right because each of the three listed statements is a correct property of the normal curve. Exam trap: students confuse 2 SD (95.44 per cent) with 1.96 SD (exactly 95 per cent) and wrongly reject statement C.", "topic": "Properties of the normal curve", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Correlation refers to:", "options": {"a": "The causal relationship between two variables", "b": "The association between two variables", "c": "The proportion of variance that two variables share", "d": "A statistical method that can only be used with a correlational research design"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: correlation refers to the association between two variables. Correlation describes the degree and direction of the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Francis Galton (1888) introduced the idea, and Karl Pearson (1896) worked out the product-moment coefficient, r, which runs from minus 1 (perfect negative) through 0 (no linear relationship) to plus 1 (perfect positive). A positive correlation means high scores on one variable go with high scores on the other; a negative one means high goes with low. Correlation does not show causation: a third variable may drive both, or the direction may be the reverse. The proportion of variance two variables share is r squared, the coefficient of determination. Example: Riya finds that hours of sleep and exam marks correlate at r = 0.6 in her class; the two are associated and share 36 per cent of their variance, but she cannot say sleep caused the marks, since conscientious students may both sleep regularly and study more. Option b is right because correlation is, by definition, a measure of association. Exam trap: option c sounds technical and almost right, but shared variance is r squared, a quantity derived from the correlation, not what correlation itself refers to.", "topic": "Meaning of correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a dimension of psychological tests?", "options": {"a": "Reliable", "b": "Valid", "c": "Standardized", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: reliability, validity and standardisation are all essential dimensions of a psychological test. Anne Anastasi, in her textbook Psychological Testing (first published 1954), defined a psychological test as 'essentially an objective and standardized measure of a sample of behavior'. A good test must have several qualities. Reliable: it gives consistent scores, so a person tested twice, or on two halves or forms, scores about the same; this is checked by test-retest, parallel forms, split-half and coefficient alpha. Valid: it measures what it claims to measure, shown by content, criterion-related and construct validity. Standardised: it is given and scored in the same way for everyone, with fixed instructions, time limits and scoring rules, and has norms from a large representative sample. Good tests are also objective (scorers agree) and practicable (reasonable time and cost). Example: a memory test Kabir uses is reliable if his clients score about the same a week later, valid if the scores predict real forgetfulness at home, and standardised if every examiner reads the same instructions and compares results with the same norms. Option d is right because each listed property is a necessary dimension of a sound test, and a test lacking any one is defective. Exam trap: the options are written as adjectives, which can make the item look odd, but each names a property every good test must have.", "topic": "Characteristics of a good test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following means clearly determining the details of procedures of administration and interpretation of test?", "options": {"a": "Reliability", "b": "Validity", "c": "Standardization", "d": "Interview"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: standardisation means clearly fixing the details of the procedures for administering, scoring and interpreting a test. Anne Anastasi's textbook Psychological Testing (first published 1954) describes standardisation as uniformity of procedure in administering and scoring a test. The test maker lays down exactly which materials are used, the exact wording of the instructions, the time limits, how to answer an examinee's questions, how to demonstrate sample items and how each response is scored. The test is then given in this fixed way to a large, representative standardisation (norm) sample, whose scores become the norms against which any later score is interpreted. Only when everyone is tested under the same conditions can two people's scores, or one person's scores on two occasions, be fairly compared. Example: when Arjun gives a standardised intelligence test, he reads the manual's instructions word for word, times each subtest with a stopwatch and scores the answers by the manual; then he converts the raw score to an IQ using the Indian norms. If he added his own hints, the norms would no longer apply to that score. Option c is right because fixing the details of administration and interpretation is the definition of standardisation. Exam trap: reliability and validity are qualities a test shows once it is used; standardisation is the procedure that makes those scores comparable in the first place.", "topic": "Test standardisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A high correlation between two halves of a test shows the degree of internal consistency. It is known as:", "options": {"a": "Retest reliability", "b": "Split-half reliability", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: a high correlation between two halves of a test shows split-half reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. Split-half reliability is estimated from a single sitting: the test is given once, divided into two comparable halves (usually odd-numbered versus even-numbered items, which balances fatigue and difficulty), and the two half-scores are correlated. A high correlation means the items measure the same thing consistently, so it is an index of internal consistency. Because each half is only half as long, and shorter tests are less reliable, the half-test correlation is stepped up to full length with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula of Charles Spearman (1910) and William Brown (1910): full r = 2r / (1 + r). Example: Meera's 40-item anxiety scale shows a correlation of 0.60 between odd and even items; the full-test reliability is 1.20 / 1.60 = 0.75. Other internal consistency indices are Phillip Rulon's (1939) formula, the KR-20 and KR-21 formulas of Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937), and Lee Cronbach's (1951) coefficient alpha. Option b is right because correlating two halves of one test is the defining step of split-half reliability. Exam trap: test-retest reliability also uses a correlation, but between two administrations of the whole test separated in time, so it shows stability, not internal consistency.", "topic": "Split-half reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the extent to which the test measures what it purports to measure?", "options": {"a": "Validity", "b": "Reliability", "c": "Standardization", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: validity is the extent to which a test measures what it purports to measure. A test can be very consistent and still measure the wrong thing, so validity asks the more important question: does the test actually measure the trait it claims to measure, and do its scores support the decisions made from them? Truman Kelley (1927) put it this way: 'the problem of validity is that of whether a test really measures what it purports to measure'. Validity is specific to a purpose and a group, and it is a matter of degree. The main kinds of evidence are content validity (the items fairly sample the area, as a maths exam that covers the whole syllabus); criterion-related validity, either concurrent (scores agree with a present criterion) or predictive (scores forecast a future one); and construct validity, set out in a classic paper by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) and shown by convergent and discriminant evidence. Face validity, merely looking valid to test takers, is not real evidence. A test cannot be valid unless it is reliable, but a reliable test need not be valid. Example: Kabir's bathroom scale that always reads 2 kg too high is consistent (reliable) but does not give his true weight (not valid). Option a is right because 'measures what it purports to measure' is the textbook definition of validity. Exam trap: reliability (consistency) is the usual distractor; consistency is necessary for validity but is not the same thing.", "topic": "Definition of validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is also used for diagnosis and treatment of behaviour problems?", "options": {"a": "Behaviour tests", "b": "Interview", "c": "Rating scale", "d": "Questionnaires"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the interview is used both for the diagnosis and for the treatment of behaviour problems. An interview is a purposeful face-to-face conversation in which one person gathers information from, or helps, another. In diagnosis, the clinical (diagnostic) interview is the main tool: the intake interview, the case history, the mental status examination and structured or semi-structured interviews such as the Structured Clinical Interview for DSM disorders (SCID) collect symptoms, history and present functioning, while the interviewer also observes appearance, speech and mood. In treatment, the interview is itself the vehicle of change: counselling and psychotherapy sessions are therapeutic interviews in which the client is listened to, understood and helped to see problems differently and try new ways of coping. Example: Riya, a clinical psychologist, first interviews Arjun to understand his panic attacks and reach a diagnosis, then meets him weekly in therapeutic interviews to teach him breathing and coping skills. Option b is right because the interview is the one technique listed that serves both assessment and therapy. Exam trap: rating scales and questionnaires are also used in clinical work, but only to measure or screen; they gather data and do not themselves treat anything.", "topic": "Interview in diagnosis and treatment", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A popular projective test Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by Harvard Psychologist and personality theorist named:", "options": {"a": "Cattell", "b": "Melvin Lee", "c": "Philip Zimbardo", "d": "Henry Murray"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by the Harvard psychologist and personality theorist Henry Murray. Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) introduced the TAT at the Harvard Psychological Clinic, and Murray published the standard manual in 1943. The test has 31 cards: 30 ambiguous pictures of people in various situations and one blank card (card 16), for which the person imagines a picture. The examiner uses a selection of cards (Murray suggested 20 over two sessions; clinicians often use fewer), and for each the person tells a story: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. It is a projective test: because the pictures are ambiguous, the stories are assumed to reveal the teller's own needs, conflicts and motives. Stories are analysed with Murray's need-press theory from Explorations in Personality (1938): needs (such as achievement, affiliation and dominance) are inner forces, and press is the environmental force that helps or hinders them. Example: if Kabir tells story after story in which heroes keep failing despite great effort, the clinician may infer a strong need for achievement with a low expectation of success. Option d is right because Murray, a Harvard personality theorist, developed the TAT with Morgan. Exam trap: the Rorschach inkblot test of Hermann Rorschach (1921) is the other famous projective test; do not swap the two, and remember that the TAT is a story-telling (construction) technique, not an inkblot test.", "topic": "TAT and Henry Murray", "fig": "Henry Murray"}, {"q": "The Rorschach Test is developed by the famous psychiatrist:", "options": {"a": "E. Rorschach", "b": "Hermann Rorschach", "c": "L.F. Rorschach", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Rorschach Inkblot Test was developed by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach (1884 to 1922). As a schoolboy he was nicknamed \"Klecks\" (German for inkblot) because of his love of klecksography, the game of making pictures from inkblots. As a psychiatrist in Switzerland he tried his blots on about 300 psychiatric patients and 100 other people, and he published ten of them in Psychodiagnostik (1921). He died the next year, aged 37. The ten bilaterally symmetrical cards are always shown in the same order: five achromatic (I, IV, V, VI, VII), two black and red (II, III) and three multicoloured (VIII, IX, X). First the client says what each blot might be (the response phase); then, in the inquiry, the examiner asks where and why each answer was seen. Responses are scored for location (whole or detail), determinants (form, colour, shading, movement) and content. Because the blots have no fixed meaning, the test is projective: the person is thought to project needs, conflicts and ways of seeing onto them. Scoring systems multiplied until John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) unified them; the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS) followed in 2011. Example: Arjun sees \"two people dancing\" on card III, a human movement response. Option b is right because Hermann Rorschach is the psychiatrist who created and published the inkblot test. Exam trap: \"E.\" and \"L.F.\" are made-up initials meant to test whether you know the full name; and do not mix up the two classic projective tests, since the inkblots are Hermann Rorschach's while the Thematic Apperception Test is Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray's (1935).", "topic": "Rorschach inkblot test author", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Misperception can be a difficulty. For this reason which of the following are sometimes used?", "options": {"a": "Rating scales", "b": "Personality questionnaires", "c": "Behavioural assessments", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: rating scales are used because observers can misperceive what they see. Direct observation, watching a person's behaviour in a real or set-up situation, is a basic way to assess personality, and so is the interview. Both have a weakness: the observer can misperceive or misread what happens. One striking trait can colour every other judgement (the halo effect, named by Edward Thorndike in 1920), the observer's own expectations can bias what is noticed, and some traits get overlooked while others are exaggerated. A rating scale is a list of personality traits or aspects of behaviour, each with a graded scale (for example 1 to 5 from 'never' to 'very often'), that the observer must judge one by one. Because it forces attention onto every listed trait in turn, it limits the chance of overlooking some traits and exaggerating others, which reduces misperception. Example: instead of writing a loose impression of Kabir after a group task, the psychologist rates him on cooperation, leadership, anxiety and irritability on the same 5-point scale. Option a is right because introductory textbooks present rating scales as the tool used in direct observation to cut down observers' misperceptions. Exam trap: rating scales do not remove bias completely; raters still show halo, leniency and central tendency errors, which is why behavioural assessment, counting actual behaviours, is offered as a further alternative. Key note: behavioural assessment also gets round misperception by counting actual behaviours, so c is tempting; but the stem follows Dennis Coon and John Mitterer's Introduction to Psychology, where observers' misperception is the stated reason rating scales are used, so a stands.", "topic": "Rating scales and observer misperception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered as a branch of applied psychology?", "options": {"a": "Social psychology", "b": "Personality psychology", "c": "Counselling psychology", "d": "Cognitive psychology"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: counselling psychology is a branch of applied psychology. Psychology has two broad kinds of field. Basic (pure) fields build knowledge and theory for its own sake: social, personality, cognitive, developmental, physiological and experimental psychology. Applied fields use that knowledge to solve the practical problems of real people: clinical, counselling, educational and school, industrial-organisational, health, forensic and sports psychology. Hugo Munsterberg (1913), author of Psychology and Industrial Efficiency, is often called a father of applied psychology. Counselling psychology grew out of the vocational guidance of Frank Parsons (1909), who wrote Choosing a Vocation, and out of Carl Rogers's (1942) Counseling and Psychotherapy. Its whole purpose is practical: helping people with adjustment, career, relationship and study problems. Example: Kabir, a college student torn between engineering and design, sees a counselling psychologist who uses interest tests and interviews to help him decide; that is psychology applied to a real-life problem. Option c is right because counselling psychology is defined by its practical, helping purpose, while the other three options are basic areas that supply the theory counsellors apply. Exam trap: every basic field has some applied uses (cognitive psychology feeds into education and design), so do not pick a field just because it sounds useful; ask whether it exists mainly to build theory or to serve clients. Key note: social psychology has applied offshoots (applied social psychology), but as a field it is listed among the basic areas, while counselling psychology is applied by definition, so c stands.", "topic": "Applied versus basic branches", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Counselling psychology differs from clinical psychology because:", "options": {"a": "It is more concerned with treatment of patients", "b": "It is more concerned with severe behaviour pathology", "c": "It is more concerned with everyday problems in living", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: counselling psychology differs from clinical psychology because it is more concerned with everyday problems in living. The two fields overlap (both interview, assess and help people change), but their emphasis differs. Clinical psychology began when Lightner Witmer (1896) opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania. It centres on the assessment, diagnosis and treatment of psychopathology, often more severe disorders such as schizophrenia, bipolar disorder or severe depression, and its practitioners frequently work in hospitals and psychiatric clinics. Counselling psychology works mainly with relatively healthy people facing normal difficulties: adjustment to college, career choice, relationship strain, exam stress, bereavement and other life transitions. It stresses strengths, personal growth, development and prevention rather than illness. Example: Riya, who feels lost and lonely after moving to a new city for work, goes to a counselling psychologist; her cousin Arjun, who hears voices, is treated by a clinical psychologist and a psychiatrist. Option c is right because \"everyday problems in living\" is the textbook phrase for counselling's focus on people in the normal range. Exam trap: options a and b describe clinical psychology, the reverse of what the stem asks, and \"All of the above\" tempts students who know the two fields overlap; the question asks how counselling differs, so only c fits.", "topic": "Counselling versus clinical psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Community counsellors are often employed in:", "options": {"a": "Mental health organizations", "b": "Educational organizations", "c": "Depression-oriented organizations", "d": "Crisis-oriented organization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: community counsellors are often employed in mental health organisations. Community counselling (now often called clinical mental health counselling) is counselling delivered to people in their own communities rather than in schools or large hospitals. Typical employers are community mental health centres, outpatient clinics, family service agencies, substance-use programmes, rehabilitation agencies and non-governmental organisations. In the USA, President John F. Kennedy's Community Mental Health Centers Act (1963) funded local centres meant to replace large state hospitals, building the network where such counsellors work. Community psychology then turned towards prevention: Gerald Caplan (1964) brought primary, secondary and tertiary prevention into mental health. In India, the District Mental Health Programme (1996) plays a similar role at district level. Example: Kabir works for a community mental health centre in Pune; he runs a support group for carers, counsels clients referred by local doctors and gives awareness talks in schools. Option a is right because mental health organisations are the broad and most common setting for community counsellors. Exam trap: \"educational organisations\" is the setting of school counsellors, a different specialty, and \"depression-oriented organisations\" is an invented category built from a disorder name. Key note: crisis centres and hotlines do employ community counsellors, so d is not absurd; but the broad, most common setting is mental health organisations, so a stands.", "topic": "Community counsellors' work settings", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What are the two most common clinical symptoms presented when a client voluntarily requests Counselling services?", "options": {"a": "Depression and mania", "b": "Substance abuse and anxiety", "c": "Anxiety and depression", "d": "Neurosis and psychosis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: anxiety and depression are the two most common clinical symptoms in people who request counselling voluntarily. Surveys of counselling centres, college counselling services and primary care repeatedly place anxiety (worry, nervousness, panic, stress) and depression (low mood, hopelessness, loss of interest, fatigue) at the top of the presenting problems. The World Health Organization groups them as the \"common mental disorders\" because they are so widespread, and they often occur together in the same person. Both cause enough distress to push someone to seek help, yet they usually leave enough insight and day-to-day functioning for the person to recognise the problem and come on his or her own. Example: Meera, a final-year student, books a session herself because she cannot stop worrying about placements and has felt flat and tired for weeks; her constant worry is anxiety, her flatness and fatigue depression. Option c is right because anxiety and depression are the commonest and most easily self-recognised distress states, so they dominate voluntary requests for counselling. Exam trap: \"neurosis and psychosis\" sounds like the two main kinds of disorder, but they are broad old categories, not symptoms, and people with psychosis seldom refer themselves because insight is often poor.", "topic": "Commonest presenting problems in counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The cultural, organizational, community, interpersonal, or personal dynamics that should be considered when making ethical and professional issues are referred as:", "options": {"a": "Personal considerations", "b": "Ethical considerations", "c": "Contextual considerations", "d": "Environmental considerations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the cultural, organisational, community, interpersonal or personal dynamics that should be weighed in ethical and professional decisions are called contextual considerations. Counselling ethics texts teach that a sound decision draws on three things: the ethical code (principles such as beneficence, non-maleficence, autonomy, justice and fidelity), professional standards of practice, and the context of the particular case. Context covers the client's and the counsellor's cultures, the agency's policies, the community's norms, the relationships among the people involved and the counsellor's own values. The same act can be right in one context and harmful in another, so the written code alone is not enough. Example: Riya, a counsellor in a small town, is asked to see her neighbour's son. In a city she would simply refer him elsewhere to avoid a dual relationship; in her town she may be the only counsellor, so she weighs community ties, confidentiality and the lack of alternatives before deciding how to proceed safely. Option c is right because \"contextual\" is the umbrella word for all five kinds of dynamics listed in the stem. Exam trap: \"ethical considerations\" sounds right because the stem mentions ethics, but it names the issues themselves (confidentiality, informed consent, competence), not the surrounding dynamics that shape how they apply.", "topic": "Contextual considerations in ethics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Systematic desensitisation is an effective therapy for which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Specific phobias", "b": "Maladaptive behaviours that are harmful to the individual", "c": "Mentally ill people who do not readily communicate with others", "d": "Generalised fears or anxieties"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: systematic desensitisation is most effective for specific phobias. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built the method on reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible responses cannot occur together, so deep relaxation evoked in the presence of a feared stimulus weakens the bond between that stimulus and anxiety, a form of counterconditioning. It has three steps. First, relaxation training, usually the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, an anxiety hierarchy: 10 to 20 feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, each rated on the Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, desensitisation proper: while relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when it no longer arouses anxiety. Example: Arjun, terrified of dogs, relaxes and pictures a dog far down the street, then a dog on a lead three metres away, and so on, until he can imagine patting one calmly. Option a is right because a specific phobia has a clear, identifiable trigger that can be broken into graded steps. Exam trap: \"generalised fears or anxieties\" sounds as if it should suit an anxiety therapy too, but free-floating anxiety has no clear trigger, so no hierarchy can be built.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation and phobias", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Rational-emotive therapy asserts which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Self-blame is the beginning of accepting responsibility for personal failures", "b": "Psychological problems are a result of a person's belief system", "c": "An empathetic relationship between client and therapist is essential for treatment to be successful", "d": "A directive, argumentative approach is counterproductive"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: rational-emotive therapy asserts that psychological problems result from a person's belief system. Albert Ellis (1955) began rational therapy, later renamed rational-emotive therapy (RET) and, in 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). Its core is the ABC model. A is the Activating event, B the Belief about it and C the emotional and behavioural Consequence. Common sense says A causes C; Ellis held that B is the main cause. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands (\"I must succeed\", \"others must treat me fairly\"), awfulising (\"it would be terrible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and global self-rating (\"I am worthless\"). Therapy adds D, Disputing the belief, and E, an Effective new belief. Example: Kabir and Riya both fail a mock test (A). Kabir thinks \"one mock does not decide my future\" and feels disappointed but keeps studying; Riya thinks \"I must score well or I'm a failure\" and becomes depressed (C). Same A, different B, different C. Option b is right because Ellis placed the cause of emotional disturbance in irrational beliefs, not in events. Exam trap: REBT is itself directive and argumentative (the therapist actively disputes beliefs), so option d reverses the therapy; and Ellis treated self-blame as an irrational belief, teaching unconditional self-acceptance instead.", "topic": "REBT and the belief system", "fig": "Albert Ellis"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an advantage of group therapy not found in individual therapy?", "options": {"a": "Participants gain insight into their own problems by listening to the experiences of others", "b": "Participants are often more motivated to strive to improve because lack of progress cannot be hidden from other group members", "c": "All types of group therapy depend on interactions between participants, thus no one may remain silent", "d": "The obligation to maintain confidentiality is reduced because information is shared by all participants"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: participants gain insight into their own problems by listening to the experiences of others, an advantage individual therapy cannot offer. Irvin Yalom, in The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy (ten 'curative factors' in 1970, eleven from the 1975 second edition), listed eleven therapeutic factors: instillation of hope, universality, imparting information, altruism, corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, development of socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness, catharsis and existential factors. Several depend on hearing other members. Universality is the relief of discovering that others share one's problem. Imitative behaviour means modelling how others cope. Interpersonal learning means seeing one's own patterns reflected in how others react. A single therapist cannot supply these, because they need several people with similar struggles. Groups are also cheaper and give a safe place to practise social skills. Example: Meera, in a group for social anxiety, hears Arjun describe rehearsing every sentence before he speaks; she realises she does the same, and his progress gives her hope. Option a is right because learning from other members' stories is built into groups and absent from one-to-one work. Exam trap: options b, c and d sound like features of groups but are false: members can hide or stay silent, and the duty of confidentiality is not reduced.", "topic": "Advantages of group therapy", "fig": "Irvin Yalom"}, {"q": "What alternative to the experimental method do we have?", "options": {"a": "Method of systematic observation", "b": "Survey method", "c": "Case study method", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the method of systematic observation is the alternative to the experimental method. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable, controls other variables and measures a dependent variable, so cause and effect can be inferred. Many questions cannot be studied that way, because manipulating the variable would be impossible or unethical: no one can assign children to divorced homes or to poverty. Introductory textbooks therefore describe systematic observation as the main alternative: observing and recording behaviour carefully and objectively, with clear definitions and checklists, but without manipulating anything. It takes several forms: naturalistic observation (watching behaviour in its natural setting), the case method (studying one person in depth) and the survey method (asking many people about their behaviour or attitudes). The correlational method builds on such observations, measuring how strongly two variables vary together. Example: Riya watches children in a school playground for a month, recording each act of sharing and aggression on a fixed checklist; she changes nothing, so her study is systematic observation. Option a is right because it is the umbrella name for the non-experimental approach in this framework. Exam trap: surveys and case studies are real non-experimental methods, so they tempt; but each is one form of systematic observation, the broader category. Key note: surveys and case studies are also non-experimental methods, so b and c are not wrong in themselves; but in this framework they are forms of systematic observation, the umbrella answer, so a stands.", "topic": "Alternatives to the experiment", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Neo-behaviourists differ from behaviourists on which of the following grounds?", "options": {"a": "Neo-behaviourists no longer argue that Psychologists must ask precise, well delineated questions", "b": "Neo-behaviourists no longer investigate stimuli", "c": "Neo-behaviourists no longer measure responses", "d": "Neo-behaviourists no longer insist that only observable phenomena be studied"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: neo-behaviourists no longer insisted that only observable phenomena be studied. Classical behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), allowed only observable stimuli and responses; consciousness, mind and inner states were banned. From about 1930 the neobehaviourists, influenced by logical positivism and by the operationism of the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927), loosened this rule. They accepted unobservable intervening variables that stand between stimulus and response, as long as each was tied by an operational definition to something that could be observed and measured. Edward Tolman (1932), in Purposive Behavior in Animals and Men, wrote of purpose and expectancies, gave such inner links the name intervening variables, and later described cognitive maps (1948); his rats showed latent learning, learning a maze without reward. Clark Hull (1943) built a hypothetico-deductive theory with drive, habit strength and reaction potential. Example: Kabir's rat runs a maze faster when hungry; Hull would explain this with a drive variable defined as \"hours without food\", unobservable in itself but anchored to an observable operation. Option d is right because accepting inferred inner variables is exactly what separated the neo-behaviourists from Watson. Exam trap: the neo-behaviourists stayed behaviourists in method, so they still studied stimuli, measured responses and asked precise questions; options a, b and c describe things they never gave up.", "topic": "Neobehaviourism versus Watson", "fig": "Edward Tolman and Clark Hull"}, {"q": "Which of the following issues of individual differences is sometimes called the nature-nurture controversy?", "options": {"a": "Heredity-environment", "b": "Heredity-physiological factor", "c": "Physiological factor-environment", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: the heredity-environment issue is sometimes called the nature-nurture controversy. It asks how far individual differences in intelligence, personality and behaviour come from inherited genes (nature) and how far from upbringing, culture and experience (nurture). Francis Galton (1874) popularised the phrase in English Men of Science: Their Nature and Nurture, a pairing already found in Shakespeare's The Tempest, and he began the study of twins to separate the two influences. The pure nurture view goes back to John Locke's (1690) blank slate and reached its peak in John B. Watson's (1925) boast, \"give me a dozen healthy infants\". Today's answer is interaction. Twin and adoption studies estimate heritability; genes and environments correlate (children seek out settings that suit their tendencies) and interact (a genetic risk may show itself only in certain environments); epigenetics shows that experience can switch genes on or off. Example: identical twins Arjun and Kabir, adopted into different families, grow to almost the same height, but their English vocabularies differ widely, tracking the number of books in each home. Option a is right because \"nature\" means heredity and \"nurture\" means environment. Exam trap: physiological factors are themselves a product of heredity and environment, not one side of the debate, so options b and c pair the wrong terms.", "topic": "Nature-nurture as heredity-environment", "fig": "Francis Galton"}, {"q": "An early stage of moral development, where children judge the \"goodness\" or \"badness\" of actions in terms of consequences they produce, is known as:", "options": {"a": "Object permanence", "b": "Subjective moral orientation", "c": "Objective moral orientation", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the early stage in which children judge actions by their consequences is objective moral orientation. Jean Piaget (1932), in The Moral Judgment of the Child, watched Swiss boys play marbles and told children paired stories. In one, John accidentally breaks 15 cups while opening a door; in the other, Henry breaks 1 cup while sneaking jam from a cupboard. Young children say John is naughtier, because he did more damage. Piaget called this early stage heteronomous morality or moral realism (about 5 to 10 years): rules come from adults and cannot be changed, badness is judged by objective consequences (objective responsibility), and the child believes in immanent justice, that wrongdoing is punished automatically. From about 10, autonomous morality brings subjective responsibility: intentions count, so Henry is naughtier, and rules become agreements that can be changed. Example: six-year-old Meera says her brother, who broke a vase while helping, deserves more punishment than a friend who broke a cup while stealing sweets. Option c is right because judging by consequences rather than intentions is the objective orientation. Exam trap: \"subjective moral orientation\" is the later, intention-based stage, the exact reverse. Key note: Indian texts label Piaget's early stage \"objective moral orientation (judgement)\" and the later one \"subjective\"; English textbooks more often say moral realism versus autonomous morality, so c stands.", "topic": "Piaget's objective moral orientation", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "Insight learning occurs when?", "options": {"a": "Insight involves a perceptual reorganization of elements in the environment", "b": "Insight does not involve a perceptual reorganization of elements in the environment", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: insight involves a perceptual reorganisation of elements in the environment. Wolfgang Kohler (1925), in The Mentality of Apes (German edition 1917), studied chimpanzees on Tenerife between 1913 and 1920. Sultan, given two short sticks, could not reach a banana outside his cage with either one. After a pause he fitted one stick into the other and raked in the fruit; other apes stacked boxes to reach bananas hung from the roof. Kohler, a Gestalt psychologist, argued that the animal suddenly saw the elements of the problem (sticks, banana, distance) as a new whole: a restructuring of the perceptual field, the \"aha\" experience. Insight solutions appear suddenly rather than gradually, are retained after a single success and transfer to similar problems. This contrasted with the cats of Edward Thorndike (1898), which escaped puzzle boxes by gradual trial and error. Example: Kabir stares at a jigsaw, gives up, then suddenly sees that two odd pieces form one shape, and never struggles with that step again. Option a is right because insight is defined as the perceptual reorganisation of the problem's elements. Exam trap: insight is sometimes confused with latent learning (Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik, 1930), which is learning without reward that shows up later; insight is a sudden restructuring of the problem.", "topic": "Köhler's insight learning", "fig": "Wolfgang Köhler"}, {"q": "Which of them is sometimes called as respondent conditioning or Pavlovian conditioning?", "options": {"a": "Reflex conditioning", "b": "Conditioned stimulus", "c": "Classical conditioning", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: classical conditioning is the procedure also called respondent conditioning or Pavlovian conditioning. Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs salivated before food arrived, at the sight of the dish or at the attendant's footsteps. He then paired a neutral stimulus (a metronome or bell) with food. Food is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS), and salivation to it is the unconditioned response (UCR). After repeated pairings the sound alone, now the conditioned stimulus (CS), produced salivation, the conditioned response (CR). The name \"respondent\" comes from B. F. Skinner (1938), who in The Behavior of Organisms separated respondent behaviour, reflexes elicited by a stimulus, from operant behaviour, emitted by the organism and shaped by its consequences. Classical conditioning explains learned fears, tastes and many emotional reactions. Example: Riya's mouth waters when she hears the pressure cooker whistle, because the sound has been paired many times with hot dal. Option c is right because \"classical\", \"Pavlovian\" and \"respondent\" are three names for the same kind of learning. Exam trap: \"conditioned stimulus\" is one part of the procedure, not its name, and \"reflex conditioning\" sounds right because Pavlov wrote of conditioned reflexes, but it is not the standard term.", "topic": "Classical, respondent, Pavlovian conditioning", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "Which of the following inhibition occurs not only when formal learning takes place between initial learning and recall, but also when ordinary waking life intervenes?", "options": {"a": "Retroactive", "b": "Proactive", "c": "Semantic", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: retroactive inhibition occurs not only when formal learning comes between learning and recall but also when ordinary waking life intervenes. Retroactive inhibition (interference) means that material or experience coming later disrupts recall of something learned earlier; the interference works backwards in time. Georg Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) named it. The classic evidence about everyday life comes from John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924). Two students learned lists of nonsense syllables and were tested after 1, 2, 4 or 8 hours, spent either asleep or in ordinary waking activity. After 8 hours of sleep they recalled over half the syllables; after 8 waking hours, only about a tenth. The same time had passed, so decay alone could not explain the difference: the events of waking life had interfered with the earlier learning. Example: Arjun memorises a new locker code in the morning; by evening, after a busy day of classes, chats and new phone numbers, he has forgotten it, though after a quiet sleep he might have kept it. Option a is right because interference from intervening activity, formal or ordinary, acts backwards on old learning. Exam trap: in proactive inhibition the interference runs forwards, older learning disrupting newer; remember PRO = old disrupts new, RETRO = new disrupts old.", "topic": "Retroactive inhibition and waking life", "fig": "John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach"}, {"q": "Read carefully the following statements and give appropriate answer: (i) In his writings, Maslow emphasized that self-actualization is a process, not a goal or end point. (ii) Making fuller use of personal potentials requires learning to live up to the expectations of others. (iii) Maslow described peak experiences as temporary moments of self-actualization. (iv) A major characteristic of self-actualization is their interest in status and personal recognition. (v) According to Maslow, wishful thinking and distorted self-perceptions are barriers to self-actualization.", "options": {"a": "(i), (iii) and (v) are true, but (ii) and (iv) are false", "b": "(i), (iii) and (v) are false, but (ii) and (iv) are true", "c": "All the above statements are true", "d": "All the above statements are false"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: statements (i), (iii) and (v) are true, and (ii) and (iv) are false. Abraham Maslow (1954), in Motivation and Personality, placed self-actualisation, becoming everything one is capable of becoming, at the top of his hierarchy of needs. He studied people he judged self-actualised, such as Abraham Lincoln, Eleanor Roosevelt and Albert Einstein. (i) True: Maslow stressed that self-actualisation is an ongoing process of growth, not a finish line. (ii) False: self-actualisers are autonomous and resist enculturation; they follow inner standards rather than living up to others' expectations. (iii) True: peak experiences, brief moments of awe, wonder and unity, are temporary moments of self-actualisation. (iv) False: they are problem-centred, devoted to a task or mission, and care little for status and personal recognition, which belong to the lower esteem needs. (v) True: Maslow saw defensiveness, wishful thinking, distorted self-perceptions and fear of one's own growth (the Jonah complex) as barriers. Example: Kabir, a teacher, keeps learning new methods because the work matters to him, not for awards, and feels a peak experience when a struggling class finally understands. Option a is right because it is the only option that matches this true and false pattern. Exam trap: statement (ii) sounds like healthy social adjustment, but Maslow's self-actualisers are marked by independence from social expectations.", "topic": "Maslow's self-actualisation statements", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "Emotions and their expression are modified to a great extent with:", "options": {"a": "Motivation and experience", "b": "Perception and experience", "c": "Learning and experience", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: emotions and their expression are modified to a great extent by learning and experience. Part of emotion is innate. Charles Darwin (1872), in The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals, argued that facial expressions evolved and are shared across humans and other animals. Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1971) showed that the basic expressions (happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise, disgust) are recognised across cultures: even the isolated Fore of Papua New Guinea, who had seen no films or magazines, matched faces to emotion stories far above chance. But learning reshapes both what triggers an emotion and how it is shown. Through conditioning a neutral object can come to arouse fear, as in John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's (1920) Little Albert study. Through socialisation children learn display rules (Ekman and Friesen, 1969), cultural norms for when an emotion may be shown, masked or exaggerated. Through social referencing infants read a parent's face to decide whether to fear a stranger. Experience also refines the emotions themselves: Katharine Bridges (1932) described a newborn's general excitement splitting into distress and delight, then into specific emotions. Example: Meera cried freely at five, but at fifteen she hides tears at school because she has learned that crying in class invites teasing. Option c is right because learning and experience are what modify the innate core. Exam trap: motivation and perception interact with emotion, but they are not what moulds its development and expression.", "topic": "Learning shapes emotional expression", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to whom, there must be a different set of internal and external bodily changes for each emotion and the individual must be able to perceive them?", "options": {"a": "Cannon-Bard", "b": "Schachter-Singer", "c": "James-Lange", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the James-Lange theory holds that each emotion has its own set of internal and external bodily changes and that the person must be able to perceive them. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble. William James (1884), in \"What is an emotion?\", and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) reversed this order. The stimulus first triggers bodily changes, internal (visceral: heart rate, breathing, the gut) and external (muscular: running, striking, crying), and the emotion is our perception of those changes. In James's words, \"we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble\". For this to work, every emotion must have its own distinct bodily pattern, or we could not tell fear from anger, and the person must be able to sense that pattern. Example: Arjun's heart pounds and his legs start running when a dog lunges at him; by the James-Lange view, his fear is the feeling of that pounding and running. Option c is right because a distinct, perceivable bodily pattern for each emotion is the defining assumption of this theory. Exam trap: Walter Cannon (1927) attacked exactly this assumption, arguing that the same visceral changes occur in different emotions, so Cannon-Bard is the critic of the idea, not its source.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion", "fig": "William James and Carl Lange"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2015": [{"q": "Spearman said that there was a general ability employed by people while adjusting with different sorts of intellectual tasks, these factors are called the:", "concept": "The answer is b: Charles Spearman (1904) called the single ability that people use across every kind of intellectual task the general factor, or g. He noticed that schoolchildren who did well in one subject or test tended to do well in the others: all the scores correlated positively, a pattern later called the positive manifold. To explain it he developed an early form of factor analysis and proposed his two-factor theory. Every task draws on g, a common mental energy used in reasoning, comprehension and problem solving, plus a specific factor, s, that belongs to that one task alone (a special knack for arithmetic or for music). Because g is shared, it explains why different tests correlate; because each s is private to one test, it explains why those correlations are never perfect. For example, Arjun scores high in mathematics, vocabulary and puzzles alike; Spearman would credit his high g, while his extra flair for music would reflect a specific factor. Option b is right because the stem describes one ability employed while adjusting to different sorts of intellectual tasks, which is exactly the general factor. Exam trap: the stem says 'factors' in the plural, which pushes students towards 'specific factors' or 'multi factors'; but specific factors are tied to single tasks, and the multifactor theories were the ones that rejected a single dominant g.", "traps": {"a": "Specific factors (s) are the other half of Charles Spearman's two-factor theory: abilities unique to a single task, so they cannot be the ability used across different sorts of tasks.", "c": "'Multi factors' points to the multifactor theories, such as Edward Thorndike's view of intelligence as many separate abilities and Louis Thurstone's (1938) seven primary mental abilities, which rejected one dominant general ability instead of describing it.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because option b, the general factor (g), is exactly what the stem describes."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true?", "concept": "The answer is a: creativity and intelligence are two related concepts, linked but not the same. Intelligence, as IQ tests measure it, mostly rewards convergent thinking: finding the one correct answer. Creativity is producing ideas that are both novel and useful. J. P. Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, put divergent thinking at the heart of creativity: producing many varied answers, scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many categories), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how much detail). He placed both kinds of thinking inside one Structure of Intellect model, which already shows they belong together. Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) compared pupils who scored very high on creativity tests with pupils who scored very high on IQ and found the two groups overlapped only partly. The threshold hypothesis, associated with E. Paul Torrance, who built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking (1966), sums up the evidence: below an IQ of about 120 creativity and intelligence rise together, but above it they barely correlate. Overall the correlation is positive but modest. So Meera needs reasonable intelligence to invent a clever science project, but having a higher IQ than Kabir does not guarantee she is more creative. Option a is right because the two abilities are correlated and overlap without being identical. Exam trap: option c sounds generous, but 'related' and 'never linked' contradict each other, so both cannot be true at once.", "traps": {"b": "This overstates the difference: research from J. P. Guilford onwards finds a positive, though modest, correlation between creativity and intelligence, so 'never linked' is false.", "c": "Options a and b contradict each other (related versus never linked), so they cannot both be true; only a is supported by the evidence.", "d": "'None of these' fails because option a is true: creativity and intelligence are related, with a positive but modest correlation."}}, {"q": "Social Psychology gives emphasis on the individual's:", "concept": "The answer is a: social psychology gives emphasis to the individual's behaviour in social situations. Social psychology is the scientific study of how a person's behaviour, thought and feeling are shaped by other people. Gordon Allport (1897 to 1967) gave the classic definition: it tries to understand and explain how the thought, feeling and behaviour of individuals are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of others. Robert Baron and Donn Byrne, in their long-running textbook, call it the scientific field that seeks to understand the nature and causes of individual behaviour and thought in social situations, and older Indian textbooks define it simply as the scientific study of the behaviour of the individual in social situations. Two points follow. The unit of study is the individual (sociology studies the group or society as a whole), and the focus is behaviour that can be observed and measured. For example, Riya speaks freely with friends but stays silent in a large seminar; a social psychologist studies how the audience changes her behaviour. Option a is right because the standard definitions put the individual's behaviour in social settings at the centre. Exam trap: 'mind' and 'phenomenology' sound deeper, but they belong to other traditions, early structuralism and the humanistic or Gestalt description of lived experience, not the defining focus of social psychology. Key note: modern definitions add thought and feeling to behaviour, so 'mind' is not absurd; but the textbook definitions name the individual's behaviour as the core, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Phenomenology is the study of subjective conscious experience as it is lived, central to the humanistic and Gestalt traditions; it is not the defining focus of social psychology.", "c": "'Mind' was the subject of early structuralism and introspection (Wilhelm Wundt, Edward Titchener); social psychology studies thought too, but its definitions centre on the individual's behaviour in social situations.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because option a, the individual's behaviour in social situations, is what the standard definitions emphasise."}}, {"q": "Pick out the factor that does not play an important role in socialization of children.", "concept": "The answer is d: knowledge about arts and crafts does not play an important role in socialisation. Socialisation is the process by which a child learns the norms, values, roles, language and skills of the society, until they become the child's own and the child is accepted as a member. Its agents are the family (the primary agent), then school, peers, the media and religion. Its means include reward, punishment and discipline, which teach which behaviour is approved; imitation and modelling, shown in Albert Bandura's (1961) Bobo doll studies, where children copied an adult's aggression towards a doll; and identification with parents and other admired figures. Social-emotional development (attachment, self-control, empathy, reading others' feelings) is both a product of socialisation and a driver of it, because a child who understands others' feelings learns social rules faster. For example, little Kabir learns to share his toys because his mother praises him when he shares, scolds him when he snatches, and he copies his older sister. Option d is right because arts and crafts knowledge is one specific skill a child may or may not pick up; it does not shape how the child learns society's norms and roles. Exam trap: 'punishment and discipline' sounds negative, so students pick it as the odd one out, but discipline is one of the main means by which socialisation works.", "traps": {"a": "The family and close personal influences are the primary agent of socialisation, the first and strongest source of a child's norms and values.", "b": "Punishment and discipline, along with reward, are core means of socialisation: they teach the child which behaviour society approves.", "c": "Social-emotional development (attachment, empathy, self-control) is both a product and a driver of socialisation, so it does play an important role."}}, {"q": "Group dynamic is a system of behaviour or psychological process occurring:", "concept": "The answer is c: group dynamics covers behaviour and psychological processes both within a social group and between social groups. Kurt Lewin (1890 to 1947), often called the father of modern social psychology, coined the term and in 1945 founded the Research Center for Group Dynamics at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. The standard definition calls group dynamics a system of behaviours and psychological processes occurring within a social group (intragroup dynamics) or between social groups (intergroup dynamics). Within a group it covers cohesion, roles, norms, leadership, conformity and decision making. Between groups it covers competition, cooperation, prejudice and conflict. Muzafer Sherif's Robbers Cave study (1954) showed the intergroup side clearly: boys at a summer camp, split into two groups, turned hostile through competition and came together again only through goals that needed both groups. For example, Arjun's college cricket team develops its own norms and chooses a captain (within the group), and then builds a fierce rivalry with the neighbouring college's team (between groups): both are group dynamics. Option c is right because the definition explicitly includes both levels. Exam trap: each single option is half of the definition, and option a looks complete because 'group dynamics' sounds as if it means one group, but intergroup processes are equally part of the field.", "traps": {"a": "Intragroup processes (cohesion, roles, norms, leadership) are only half of group dynamics; choosing a alone leaves out the processes between groups.", "b": "Intergroup processes (competition, prejudice, conflict) are only the other half; choosing b alone leaves out what happens within a group.", "d": "'None of these' fails because option c, processes both within and between groups, matches the standard definition."}}, {"q": "\"Communication is a process of meaningful interaction among human beings.\" This definition has been given by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the definition 'communication is a process of meaningful interaction among human beings' was given by Dalton E. McFarland, an American management writer, in his management textbook. He added that communication is the process by which meanings are perceived and understandings are reached among people. Two phrases carry the idea: 'process' (it unfolds over time, it is not a single act) and 'meaningful interaction' (both sides take part and share meaning). The basic elements of communication are a sender who encodes an idea, the message, a channel (speech, print, a phone screen), a receiver who decodes it, feedback that returns to the sender, and noise, anything that distorts the message on the way. Management texts often print McFarland's definition beside that of Louis A. Allen, who called communication the sum of all the things one person does when he wants to create understanding in the mind of another, 'a bridge of meaning'. For example, when Riya explains a new duty rota to her team and they ask questions until everyone understands it, that two-way exchange of meaning is communication in McFarland's sense. Option b is right because the 'meaningful interaction among human beings' wording is McFarland's. Exam trap: Allen's definition is often quoted next to it, so students mix up the 'bridge of meaning' line with the 'meaningful interaction' line.", "traps": {"a": "Louis A. Allen, another management writer, defined communication as the sum of all the things one person does to create understanding in the mind of another, 'a bridge of meaning', not as 'meaningful interaction among human beings'.", "c": "Brown is another name that appears in lists of communication definitions, but the 'meaningful interaction among human beings' wording is not his; it is Dalton E. McFarland's.", "d": "Kelly also appears in such lists of definitions, but this definition belongs to Dalton E. McFarland, not to Kelly."}}, {"q": "Characteristics of the source which have been shown to increase attitude change are:", "concept": "The answer is b: high credibility and high attractiveness are the source characteristics shown to increase attitude change. In the 1950s Carl Hovland led the Yale Communication Research Program; his book with Irving Janis and Harold Kelley, Communication and Persuasion (1953), broke persuasion into who says what to whom: the source, the message and the audience. Two source factors stand out. Credibility combines expertise (the source knows the facts) and trustworthiness (the source has no reason to mislead). Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss (1951) gave students the same article on atomic submarines, credited either to the physicist Robert Oppenheimer or to the Soviet newspaper Pravda; the high-credibility version changed more opinions, although four weeks later the gap had almost vanished, the sleeper effect. Attractiveness covers likeability, physical attractiveness and similarity to the audience; Shelly Chaiken (1979) found that physically attractive student communicators persuaded more people to sign a petition. For example, Meera is more likely to switch toothpaste on the advice of a dentist she likes than on the word of an anonymous stranger. Option b is right because it is a high level of each trait, not merely its presence, that raises persuasion. Exam trap: options c and d pair high with low on the same trait, which describes no real source and is meaningless. Key note: option a names the same two traits; what actually increases attitude change is a high level of each, so b is the more exact answer and stands. If a source lists a, it is the same idea.", "traps": {"a": "Credibility and attractiveness are the right two traits, but the option does not say at what level; it is high credibility and high attractiveness that increase attitude change, so b is the more exact answer.", "c": "This pairs high and low attractiveness, a contradiction that describes no real source; attractiveness helps persuasion only when it is high.", "d": "This pairs high and low credibility, another contradiction; Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss (1951) showed that the high-credibility source produced more opinion change."}}, {"q": "Common features of prejudice do not include:", "concept": "The answer is c: rational and flexible opinions are not a feature of prejudice; they are its opposite. Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, defined ethnic prejudice as an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalisation, which may be felt or expressed, towards a whole group or towards an individual because he is a member of that group. Two words carry the meaning: faulty (it is not based on accurate evidence) and inflexible (it resists contrary evidence). Prejudice is usually described with three components, the ABC model: affective (negative feelings such as dislike, fear or contempt), cognitive (stereotypes and prejudgement of group members before knowing them as individuals) and behavioural (discrimination and stereotyped actions towards them). For example, Arjun refuses to rent his flat to students from another state (behaviour) because he believes they are all noisy (stereotype) and feels irritated at the very thought (feeling); when a quiet, tidy student from that state applies, he dismisses her as an exception, which shows how rigid prejudice is. Option c is right because prejudice is irrational and rigid, the reverse of rational and flexible opinion. Exam trap: the stem's 'do not include' is easy to miss; the other three options each describe one genuine component of prejudice.", "traps": {"a": "Negative feelings are the affective component of prejudice, so they are a genuine feature of it.", "b": "Stereotyped behaviours, such as discrimination and avoidance, are the behavioural component of prejudice.", "d": "Prejudgement about members of a group is the cognitive component (stereotypes); the word 'prejudice' itself means prejudgement."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true for interpersonal attraction?", "concept": "The answer is c: both personality characteristics and the environment play a role in interpersonal attraction. Interpersonal attraction is a positive attitude towards another person, the wish to be near them and to like them. Its causes are usually sorted into two kinds. Personal factors lie in the people themselves: physical attractiveness (in Elaine Walster and colleagues' 1966 computer dance study, looks best predicted whether students wanted another date), similarity of attitudes and values (Donn Byrne, The Attraction Paradigm, 1971), and reciprocity, liking those who like us. Situational or environmental factors lie in the setting: proximity, shown by Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) in Westgate West, married-student housing at MIT, where next-door neighbours were named as close friends far more often than people further down the corridor; mere exposure, Robert Zajonc's (1968) finding that repeated contact alone increases liking; and arousal, shown in Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron's (1974) suspension bridge study, where men on a frightening bridge were more attracted to a female interviewer. For example, Riya and Kabir become close friends because they sit next to each other in class (proximity) and discover they share a love of cricket (similarity). Option c is right because attraction depends on the people and the situation together. Exam trap: the word 'only' in options a and b is the giveaway; each names one real set of causes but excludes the other.", "traps": {"a": "Personal characteristics (looks, similarity, reciprocity) do matter, but 'only' wrongly excludes situational causes such as proximity and mere exposure.", "b": "Environmental factors (proximity, exposure, arousal) do matter, but 'only' wrongly excludes personal factors such as physical attractiveness and similarity.", "d": "'None of these' fails because option c is true: personal characteristics and the environment both shape attraction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following may cause not only lasting physical injury, but reduce efficiency by causing mental fatigue and loss of mental alertness?", "concept": "The answer is b: noise can cause lasting physical injury and also reduce efficiency through mental fatigue and loss of alertness. Noise is unwanted sound, and it is the classic working-conditions factor studied in industrial and environmental psychology, alongside illumination, temperature, ventilation and music at work. Its physical effect is direct: long exposure to loud sound, roughly above 85 decibels across a working day, damages the hair cells of the inner ear and causes permanent noise-induced hearing loss. Its psychological effects are subtler: noise raises stress, fatigue and irritability, breaks concentration and increases errors. David Glass and Jerome Singer (1972) showed that unpredictable, uncontrollable bursts of noise lowered people's later frustration tolerance and proofreading accuracy, even after the noise had stopped; when people believed they could switch the noise off, these after-effects shrank. For example, Arjun, who works a full shift beside a loud printing press, slowly loses some of his hearing and by the afternoon makes more mistakes and feels drained. Option b is right because the stem pairs lasting physical injury with mental fatigue and loss of alertness, the standard textbook description of the effects of noise. Exam trap: pollution also harms health, so it is tempting, but the textbook passage on mental fatigue and lost alertness at work is about noise.", "traps": {"a": "Pollution of air and water harms physical health, but the classic working-conditions passage linking lasting injury with mental fatigue and loss of alertness concerns noise.", "c": "Yoga is a practice that reduces stress and improves concentration; it causes neither injury nor mental fatigue.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because option b, noise, fits the description of lasting injury plus mental fatigue and loss of alertness."}}, {"q": "According to whom, the personality is \"the distinctive patterns of behaviour that characterize each individual's adaptation to the situation of his or her life\"?", "concept": "The answer is b: Walter Mischel defined personality as 'the distinctive patterns of behaviour (including thoughts and emotions) that characterise each individual's adaptation to the situations of his or her life'. Walter Mischel (1930 to 2018) is best known for Personality and Assessment (1968), which argued that trait scores predict behaviour in any one situation only weakly and so started the person-situation debate. His definition, from his textbook Introduction to Personality, fits that view: it stresses patterns of behaviour and adaptation to situations rather than fixed inner traits. Compare Gordon Allport (1937), whose famous definition calls personality 'the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment'. Allport's wording points inside the person (organisation, psychophysical systems); Mischel's points to observable patterns across life situations. Mischel later described these patterns as stable if-then profiles: if the situation is X, the person does A; if it is Y, the person does B. For example, Meera is talkative with friends but quiet with elders, and reliably so; for Mischel that stable pattern of adapting to situations is her personality. Option b is right because the quoted words, 'distinctive patterns of behaviour' and 'adaptation to the situations of his or her life', are Mischel's. Exam trap: Allport's definition also mentions adjustment to the environment, so students confuse the two; look for 'dynamic organisation' (Allport) versus 'distinctive patterns of behaviour' (Mischel).", "traps": {"a": "Gordon Allport (1937) defined personality as 'the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment', a different wording that stresses inner organisation.", "c": "Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) did the lexical study that found about 18,000 trait words in the dictionary, about 4,500 of them stable traits; they did not give this definition.", "d": "Charles Potkay and Bem Allen wrote a personality textbook; the 'distinctive patterns of behaviour' definition is Walter Mischel's, not theirs."}}, {"q": "The view of Freud \"males are dominant or superior to females\" is rejected by:", "concept": "The answer is c: Karen Horney rejected Freud's view that males are naturally superior to females. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) held that a girl discovers she lacks a penis, feels penis envy and inferiority, and develops a weaker superego than a boy; for him, female inferiority was rooted in anatomy. Karen Horney (1885 to 1952), a German psychoanalyst who later worked in the USA, challenged this from 1922 onwards. She argued that the idea of penis envy was drawn from neurotic patients and seen through male eyes, and she proposed that men may feel womb envy, envy of women's power to bear children, which they sublimate into achievement. Women's feelings of inferiority, she said, come from their social and cultural subordination, such as economic dependence and narrow roles, not from biology. Her essays were later collected as Feminine Psychology (1967). For example, if Riya feels less capable than her brother, Horney would look at a family that gave him all the opportunities, not at her body. Option c is right because Horney is the theorist textbooks credit with directly rejecting Freud's idea of female inferiority. Exam trap: Anna Freud sounds plausible because she was a famous woman analyst, but she extended her father's theory loyally instead of attacking it. Key note: Alfred Adler also attacked the cultural overvaluation of men (his 'masculine protest'), so a is not absurd; but Horney is the theorist textbooks credit with directly rejecting Freud's view of female inferiority, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) did criticise the cultural overvaluation of men through his idea of the masculine protest, but textbooks credit Karen Horney with the direct rejection of Freud's view of female inferiority.", "b": "Anna Freud (The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, 1936) extended her father's ego psychology and stayed loyal to his theory; she did not reject his view of women.", "d": "'None of these' fails because option c is correct: Karen Horney directly rejected Freud's view of female inferiority."}}, {"q": "In Adler's view, the main driving force in personality is:", "concept": "The answer is a: for Alfred Adler the main driving force in personality is the striving for superiority. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), an early colleague of Sigmund Freud who broke away in 1911, founded individual psychology. He began with organ inferiority (his 1907 study argued that people compensate for a weak organ or body part) and then widened the idea: every child starts small and dependent, so everyone has feelings of inferiority. These feelings are not a weakness but the spur to growth. We strive to overcome them, and this striving for superiority, later called the striving for perfection or completion, is the master motive. Healthy striving is guided by social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), concern for others and the community; unhealthy striving turns into a selfish superiority complex. Each person's unique way of striving is the style of life, aimed at a fictional final goal (fictional finalism). For example, Kabir stammered as a child and worked hard to become a fine debater; Adler would see compensation for felt inferiority driving his striving. Option a is right because Adler named the striving for superiority as the one dynamic force behind behaviour. Exam trap: 'striving for inferiority' borrows Adler's own vocabulary, but inferiority feelings are what we strive away from, not a goal anyone pursues.", "traps": {"b": "'Striving for inferiority' is not a concept; in Alfred Adler's theory inferiority feelings are the starting point we strive to overcome, not the goal.", "c": "The collective unconscious is Carl Jung's inherited layer of the psyche that holds the archetypes, not Alfred Adler's driving force.", "d": "'None of these' fails because option a, the striving for superiority, is Alfred Adler's master motive."}}, {"q": "Carl Jung noted that between the ego and the outside world we often find a:", "concept": "The answer is d: Carl Jung placed the persona between the ego and the outside world. Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), founder of analytical psychology, took the word persona from the Latin for the mask an actor wore on stage. The persona is the social face or role we show to others, shaped by what society expects of us: the polite student, the confident doctor, the dutiful son. It mediates between the ego, the centre of conscious awareness, and the outer world, helping us adapt and get along. Jung counted it among the archetypes, the universal patterns of the collective unconscious, alongside the shadow (the disowned dark side), the anima and animus (the feminine side in a man and the masculine side in a woman) and the self (the archetype of wholeness). The persona is useful, but Jung warned against identifying too closely with it, so that the person loses touch with the real self behind the mask. For example, Arjun is always cheerful and obliging at the office; if he comes to believe that role is all he is, he has become over-identified with his persona. Option d is right because the persona is exactly the mask that stands between the ego and the outside world. Exam trap: the other three options are real neo-Freudian terms, but they belong to Karen Horney and Alfred Adler, not to Jung.", "traps": {"a": "Basic anxiety is Karen Horney's concept, a child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a hostile world; it is not Jung's mediator between the ego and the world.", "b": "Style of life is Alfred Adler's term for each person's unique pattern of striving towards goals, not a Jungian structure.", "c": "The feeling of superiority (and the superiority complex) belongs to Alfred Adler's individual psychology, where it arises as overcompensation for inferiority."}}, {"q": "Which of the following include phobia, panic disorder, obsessive compulsive disorder, and post-traumatic stress disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: phobia, panic disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) were grouped together as anxiety disorders. In DSM-IV-TR (2000) the anxiety disorders chapter included specific phobia, social phobia, agoraphobia, panic disorder, OCD, PTSD, acute stress disorder and generalised anxiety disorder (GAD). What united them was anxiety as the core feature: excessive fear of a particular object or situation in phobias; sudden attacks of intense fear in panic disorder; anxiety driven by intrusive thoughts and relieved by rituals in OCD; and fear and hyperarousal after a trauma in PTSD. DSM-5 (2013) reorganised the group, moving OCD into a new chapter, Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders, and PTSD into Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders, while the phobias and panic disorder stayed in the anxiety chapter. For example, Meera avoids lifts (phobia), Arjun has sudden pounding-heart attacks (panic), Kabir checks the gas knob twenty times (OCD) and Riya relives an accident in nightmares (PTSD): older texts file all four as anxiety disorders. Option a is right because anxiety is the shared core of all four conditions listed. Exam trap: the other options are real diagnostic categories, so they look plausible, but none of them contains phobias or panic. Key note: since DSM-5, of the four listed, only the phobias and panic disorder are still anxiety disorders; the item uses the older DSM-IV grouping (all four share anxiety as the core feature), and of these options anxiety disorders stands.", "traps": {"b": "Conduct disorder is a disruptive behaviour disorder of childhood (aggression, rule-breaking, violating others' rights), now in DSM-5's disruptive, impulse-control and conduct disorders chapter; it contains no phobias or panic.", "c": "Oppositional defiant disorder is a pattern of angry, argumentative and defiant behaviour in children, another disruptive behaviour disorder, not a group that includes anxiety conditions.", "d": "Mood disorders are the depressive and bipolar disorders, defined by disturbed mood rather than by anxiety."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the current version of Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of mental disorders?", "concept": "The answer is a: DSM-V, properly written DSM-5, was the current edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders in 2015. The manual is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Its editions run DSM-I (1952), DSM-II (1968), DSM-III (1980), DSM-III-R (1987), DSM-IV (1994), DSM-IV-TR (2000, a text revision) and DSM-5 (May 2013). DSM-III was the turning point: it brought explicit diagnostic criteria, a descriptive stance that is neutral about causes, and the multiaxial system of five axes. DSM-5 switched from Roman to Arabic numerals so that later updates could be numbered, dropped the multiaxial system, regrouped disorders into new chapters (OCD and the trauma disorders left the anxiety chapter) and added dimensional ratings. For example, when Kabir, a trainee clinician in 2015, diagnosed a child with autism spectrum disorder, he used DSM-5, in which older separate labels such as Asperger's disorder had been merged into one spectrum. Option a is right because DSM-5 is the newest edition among the options and was the edition in use in 2015. Exam trap: printing 'DSM-V' in Roman numerals tempts students to think it is a fake option, but it simply means DSM-5. Key note: the current edition today is DSM-5-TR (March 2022); when this paper was set DSM-5 was current, and it is still the newest edition among these options, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "DSM-IV (1994) was replaced first by its text revision, DSM-IV-TR (2000), and then by DSM-5 (2013), so it was not current.", "c": "DSM-IV-TR (2000) was only a text revision of DSM-IV, and DSM-5 superseded it in 2013.", "d": "DSM-III (1980) introduced explicit criteria and the multiaxial system, but it is several editions old."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of phobia?", "concept": "The answer is c: obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is not a type of phobia. A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object, situation or activity, out of proportion to the real danger, which the person avoids or endures with great distress. DSM-IV and ICD-10 recognise three types. Specific (simple) phobia is fear of one particular object or situation; DSM-5 lists five subtypes: animal (dogs, snakes), natural environment (heights, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying) and other. Social phobia, now also called social anxiety disorder, is fear of being watched, judged or embarrassed in social or performance situations. Agoraphobia is fear of places where escape might be difficult or help unavailable, such as crowds, public transport or being outside the home alone. OCD is a separate disorder: obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts or images (contamination, harm), and compulsions are repetitive acts or mental rituals (washing, checking, counting) performed to reduce the anxiety the obsessions cause. For example, Riya's terror of dogs is a specific phobia, while Kabir's hour of handwashing after touching a door handle, driven by intrusive thoughts of germs, is OCD. Option c is right because OCD is defined by obsessions and compulsions, not by fear of an object or situation. Exam trap: OCD involves anxiety and, until DSM-5, sat in the same anxiety chapter as the phobias, but sharing a chapter does not make it a phobia.", "traps": {"a": "Social phobia (social anxiety disorder), fear of being judged or embarrassed in social situations, is one of the three recognised types of phobia.", "b": "Specific (simple) phobia, fear of a particular object or situation such as dogs or heights, is the commonest type of phobia.", "d": "Agoraphobia, fear of places where escape or help may be hard to get, is the third recognised type of phobia."}}, {"q": "The person with generalised anxiety disorder:", "concept": "The answer is a: a person with generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) worries about everyday routine events. DSM-5 defines GAD by excessive anxiety and worry about a number of events or activities, such as work, school, health, money and ordinary daily matters, occurring more days than not for at least 6 months. The person finds the worry hard to control, and it comes with at least 3 of 6 symptoms in adults (only 1 in children): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily fatigued, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension and sleep disturbance. Because the worry is not tied to one object, older texts called it free-floating anxiety. Thomas Borkovec, from the early 1990s, proposed that worry works as cognitive avoidance: thinking about threats in words dampens the vivid, frightening images and bodily arousal of fear, so the worry is rewarded and keeps going. For example, Meera lies awake worrying whether her son reached school safely, whether the electricity bill was paid and whether a mild headache means something serious, day after day for months; that pattern is GAD. Option a is right because the hallmark of GAD is persistent, hard-to-control worry about ordinary, everyday matters. Exam trap: students sometimes assume an anxiety disorder needs a dramatic trigger, but in GAD the worry spreads over routine life, unlike the single feared object of a phobia or the sudden attacks of panic disorder.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the definition: worry about everyday events is the core feature of GAD, so its absence rules GAD out.", "c": "Everyone has everyday routines; the problem in GAD is not a lack of events but excessive, hard-to-control worry about them.", "d": "Being carefree and settled is the opposite of GAD, whose symptoms include restlessness, irritability and muscle tension."}}, {"q": "The first text book of experimental psychology \"Principles of Physiological Psychology\" was published by Wilhelm Wundt in:", "concept": "The answer is a: Wilhelm Wundt published Principles of Physiological Psychology (Grundzüge der physiologischen Psychologie) in 1874. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician who had assisted Hermann von Helmholtz, brought the book out in two parts, in 1873 and 1874, and the date is usually given as 1874, when it was complete. It is often called the first textbook of experimental psychology. In it Wundt argued that psychology should be an independent science that studies conscious experience with the experimental methods of physiology; 'physiological' meant borrowing experimental method, not reducing the mind to the body. Five years later, in 1879, his students began research in the Leipzig laboratory, the conventional birth of scientific psychology, and in 1881 he founded the journal Philosophische Studien. Students who trained with him, such as Edward Titchener and James McKeen Cattell, carried the new science to the USA. A simple timeline helps Arjun remember: book 1874, laboratory 1879, journal 1881. Option a is right because 1874 is the year the book appeared in full. Exam trap: 1920 is a real Wundt date, but it is the year he died, not the year of the book.", "traps": {"b": "1920 is the year Wilhelm Wundt died in Leipzig, not the year his textbook appeared.", "c": "1958 has no link to Wundt's book; it is, for instance, the year Joseph Wolpe published Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition.", "d": "1961 has no link to Wundt's book either; it is, for instance, the year of Albert Bandura's Bobo doll study."}}, {"q": "Other name of control variable is:", "concept": "The answer is d: a control variable is an extraneous variable that the experimenter holds constant. In an experiment the independent variable (IV) is what the experimenter manipulates, and the dependent variable (DV) is the outcome measured. An extraneous variable is any other variable that might affect the DV: participant variables such as age, intelligence and fatigue; task variables such as instructions; and environmental or situational variables such as noise, lighting and time of day. When the experimenter holds such a variable constant or otherwise neutralises it, it becomes a control variable; if it is left uncontrolled and changes along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable and spoils the causal conclusion. The techniques of control are elimination, holding conditions constant, matching, counterbalancing and random assignment. For example, Riya tests whether background music (IV) affects memory scores (DV); she runs every participant at 10 am in the same quiet room, so time of day and noise, two extraneous variables, are now control variables. Option d is right because control variables are extraneous variables brought under control, which is why the two terms are paired. Exam trap: 'manipulated variable' sounds like something the experimenter controls, but it is the other name for the IV, the one variable deliberately varied rather than held constant. Key note: some Indian texts call the extraneous variables that can affect the DV 'relevant variables', so c is close; but the standard pairing in research methods texts is that a control variable is an extraneous variable held constant, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Manipulated variable' is another name for the independent variable, the one the experimenter deliberately varies, which is the opposite of a variable held constant.", "b": "A quantitative variable is one measured in amounts (reaction time, test score), contrasted with qualitative or categorical variables; it says nothing about control.", "c": "Some Indian texts use 'relevant variables' for extraneous variables that could affect the DV, so it is close, but the standard pairing is that a control variable is an extraneous variable held constant."}}, {"q": "The key features of experimental psychology do not include:", "concept": "The answer is b: introspection is not a key feature of experimental psychology as the method is understood today. An experiment is a controlled test of a cause-and-effect idea. The researcher manipulates one variable (the independent variable), holds other conditions constant or balances them by random assignment (control over variables), and records the outcome with careful, objective and repeatable measurement. Because only the manipulated variable differs between conditions, a change in the outcome can be traced to it, so the experiment is the one method that can establish cause and effect. Example: Arjun randomly assigns 40 students to study with or without background music, keeps the room, time and word list the same, and counts the words recalled; if the music group recalls fewer, he can say the music caused the drop. Introspection, by contrast, is trained self-observation of one's own conscious experience. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) used a controlled form of it in his Leipzig laboratory, and Edward Titchener (1898) made it the core of structuralism. It fell out of favour because private reports could not be checked or agreed (the Würzburg school's imageless-thought reports clashed with Titchener's), and John B. Watson (1913) rejected it altogether. Option b is right because introspection is a subjective report of experience, not a feature of controlled, objective experimentation. Exam trap: because Wundt founded experimental psychology and used introspection, students assume introspection belongs to the experimental method; the features examiners list are control, manipulation, measurement and causal inference. Key note: Wundt's own laboratory did use controlled introspection, so the option is not absurd historically; but it is not a feature of experimental psychology as the method is taught today, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Control over variables (holding extraneous variables constant, randomising, matching) is the heart of the experiment; without it no causal claim is possible, so it is a key feature, not the exception.", "c": "Careful, objective and repeatable measurement of the dependent variable is a defining feature of the experiment, which is why it cannot be the one that is missing.", "d": "Establishing a cause-and-effect relationship is the very purpose of an experiment and the reason it is preferred over correlational and observational methods."}}, {"q": "In experimental method the variable which is manipulated, is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: the variable that is manipulated in an experiment is called the independent variable. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes one factor, sets its levels and decides which group gets which level; this is the independent variable (IV), the presumed cause. The dependent variable (DV) is the outcome that is measured to see whether it changes with the IV, the presumed effect; its value 'depends on' the IV. Extraneous variables are all the other factors that could also affect the DV, such as time of day, noise or fatigue. An extraneous variable that the researcher holds constant becomes a control variable; one that is left free and varies along with the IV becomes a confounding variable, which spoils the causal conclusion. Robert Woodworth's textbook Experimental Psychology (1938) did much to make the IV and DV terms standard in psychology. Example: Kabir gives participants 0, 100 or 200 mg of caffeine (IV) and measures their reaction time in milliseconds (DV), testing everyone at 10 a.m. so that time of day is a control variable. Option a is right because the IV is defined as the variable the experimenter manipulates or varies, independently of anything the participant does. Exam trap: students swap IV and DV; remember that you manipulate the IV and measure the DV, and that a control variable is held constant, not manipulated.", "traps": {"b": "The dependent variable is the measured outcome (reaction time in Kabir's study); it changes in response to the IV but is never manipulated by the experimenter.", "c": "An extraneous variable is any other factor, such as noise or fatigue, that could influence the DV; the experimenter tries to control it, not to manipulate it.", "d": "A control variable is an extraneous variable deliberately held constant across conditions (testing everyone at the same hour); it is kept fixed, the opposite of manipulated."}}, {"q": "Our first contact with the physical world is through:", "concept": "The answer is a: our first contact with the physical world is through sensations. Sensation is the process by which sensory receptors detect physical energy (light, sound waves, pressure, heat, chemicals) and convert it into neural signals, a step called transduction. The result is the most basic, elementary experience: a patch of red, a tone, warmth on the skin, a bitter taste. Perception comes next: the brain organises and interprets these sensations into meaningful objects and events (a red apple, a friend's voice). Sense organs such as the eye, ear and skin are the channels or gateways through which energy reaches us, and attention then selects which sensations receive further processing. Example: when Meera steps into a dark room and sees a faint glow, the raw experience of light is the sensation; recognising it as a mobile phone screen is perception. Option a is right because sensation is the first psychological event in the chain, the elementary experience on which all perception and knowledge of the world are built. Exam trap: examiners offer 'sense organs' because books say we know the world 'through our senses'; but an organ is a bodily structure, while the contact itself, as experienced, is the sensation. Key note: some books say we know the world through our sense organs, so b is close; but the contact itself is the sensation (sense organs are the gateway it comes through), so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Sense organs (eye, ear, nose, tongue, skin) are the anatomical gateways that house the receptors; they carry the contact, but the first psychological experience of the world is the sensation they produce.", "c": "Attention is the process of selecting some sensations for further processing; it acts on sensations that have already arrived, so it comes after the first contact.", "d": "Photoreceptors (the rods and cones of the retina) transduce light only, so they serve vision alone and cannot be our contact through sound, touch, taste or smell."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the monocular cue for depth perception?", "concept": "The answer is d: linear perspective, interposition and shadows are all monocular cues for depth perception. Depth cues are pieces of information the brain uses to judge distance in a three-dimensional world from flat retinal images. Binocular cues need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye gets a slightly different view, and the difference is larger for near objects) and convergence (the eyes turn inwards for near objects). Monocular cues work with one eye, and most are pictorial cues that painters use. Linear perspective: parallel lines, such as railway tracks, seem to converge as they recede. Interposition (overlap): a nearer object hides part of a farther one, so the hidden object is seen as farther. Light and shadow (shading): the way light falls and shadows are cast shows depth and whether a surface bulges or dips. Other monocular cues are relative size, texture gradient (James Gibson, 1950), aerial perspective (haze), height in the visual field, motion parallax and accommodation. Example: Kabir closes one eye while looking down his college corridor; the converging floor lines, the door half hidden by a pillar and the shadows under the benches still tell him what is near and what is far. Option d is right because each of the three listed cues works with a single eye. Exam trap: retinal disparity and convergence are the only binocular cues; examiners often slip one of them into a list of monocular cues, or offer motion parallax, which is monocular even though it needs movement.", "traps": {"a": "Linear perspective is a monocular cue, but choosing it alone ignores that interposition and shadows are monocular cues too.", "b": "Interposition (overlap) is a monocular pictorial cue, but it is not the only one listed; linear perspective and shadows qualify as well.", "c": "Shadows (light and shade) are a monocular cue, but picking only this option leaves out linear perspective and interposition, which are equally monocular."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of reaction time?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'control reaction time' is not a type of reaction time. Reaction time (RT) is the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the start of a response. Franciscus Donders (1868), a Dutch physiologist, described the three classic types. Simple RT (his a-reaction): one stimulus, one response, as when Riya presses a key the moment any light appears. Choice RT (b-reaction): several stimuli, each with its own response, as when Riya presses the left key for red and the right key for green. Discrimination RT (c-reaction, now called go/no-go): several stimuli, but only one calls for a response, as when Riya presses only for red and holds back for green. Donders' subtraction method treated the extra time as the duration of the extra mental stage: discrimination RT minus simple RT estimates the time needed to identify the stimulus, and choice RT minus discrimination RT estimates the time needed to select a response. This began mental chronometry. Simple RT is the shortest and choice RT the longest, and William Hick (1952) showed that choice RT rises with the logarithm of the number of alternatives (Hick's law). Option d is right because no standard classification lists a 'control' reaction time; the word is borrowed from experimental control to sound plausible. Exam trap: discrimination RT is real (some books call it recognition RT), so students strike it out by mistake; it is Donders' c-reaction.", "traps": {"a": "Simple reaction time (Donders' a-reaction: one stimulus, one fixed response) is the basic and fastest type, so it is a real category.", "b": "Choice reaction time (Donders' b-reaction: different stimuli each need a different response) is a standard type and the slowest of the three.", "c": "Discrimination reaction time (Donders' c-reaction or go/no-go: respond to one stimulus, withhold for others) is a recognised type used to estimate stimulus identification time."}}, {"q": "Attention is:", "concept": "The answer is a: attention is a behaviour as well as a cognitive process. Attention is the selection of some stimuli for further processing while others are ignored. William James (1890) described it as 'the taking possession by the mind, in clear and vivid form, of one out of what seem several simultaneously possible objects or trains of thought'. That is its cognitive side: focusing, selecting and holding information in awareness. But attention also has an observable side. When a sudden sound occurs, people and animals turn the head and eyes towards it, pause what they are doing and adjust their posture; Ivan Pavlov (1927) called this the 'what is it?' reflex, now known as the orienting reflex. Older texts also describe a preparatory set, the bodily readiness to receive an expected stimulus. Example: when Kabir hears his name called in a noisy canteen, he stops chewing, turns his head and fixes his eyes on the speaker (behaviour) while his mind picks out that one voice from the chatter (cognition). Option a is right because attention involves both the mental act of selection and the overt orienting and adjusting behaviour that goes with it. Exam trap: modern books define attention as a cognitive process, so students pick 'only a mental process'; the word 'only' is the trap, since attention also shows itself in behaviour. Key note: many modern textbooks simply call attention a cognitive process, so c is close; but the item counts the overt orienting behaviour that comes with it, and attention involves both, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Calling attention only a behavioural process reduces it to head and eye movements and ignores the mental selection of information that William James described.", "c": "Attention is mainly described as a mental process, but 'only' leaves out the observable orienting responses and postural adjustments that accompany it.", "d": "None of these is wrong because option a correctly states that attention is both behavioural and cognitive."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statistics provides a shorthand summary of large number of observations?", "concept": "The answer is b: descriptive statistics provide a shorthand summary of a large number of observations. Statistics in psychology has two broad branches. Descriptive statistics organise, summarise and describe the data actually collected: frequency tables and graphs, measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode), measures of variability (range, quartile deviation, standard deviation), the shape of the distribution (skewness, kurtosis) and correlation. Each turns hundreds of raw scores into a few numbers or a single picture. Inferential statistics go further: they use probability to generalise from a sample to the population it came from, with tools such as the t test, the F test (ANOVA), chi-square and confidence intervals. Example: Meera has anxiety scores from 200 students in Mysuru. Saying 'the mean is 42, the SD is 8 and the scores are slightly positively skewed' is descriptive; testing whether boys and girls in the whole state differ is inferential. Option b is right because summarising many observations in a few indices is exactly what descriptive statistics are defined to do. Exam trap: the usual rival is 'inferential statistics', which generalise rather than summarise; invented labels such as 'pure' or 'numerical' statistics are not branches at all, and the cue word 'summary' always points to descriptive statistics.", "traps": {"a": "'Pure statistics' is not a branch of statistics in psychology; the pure versus applied split belongs to mathematics and to fields of psychology, not to ways of summarising data.", "c": "'Numerical statistics' is not a recognised category; all statistics deal with numbers, so the label does not identify the summarising branch.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option b, descriptive statistics, is the branch defined as a summary of observations."}}, {"q": "A frequency distribution when represented by means of a graph makes the unwieldy data:", "concept": "The answer is a: representing a frequency distribution by a graph makes unwieldy data intelligible. A frequency distribution arranges raw scores into class intervals and shows how many cases fall in each. Even as a table it can be hard to take in, so it is often drawn as a graph. The common forms are the histogram (adjoining bars whose heights show frequencies), the frequency polygon (a line joining the midpoints of the intervals), the cumulative frequency curve or ogive (used to read medians and percentiles), and, for categories, the bar diagram and pie chart. Classic texts such as Henry Garrett's Statistics in Psychology and Education (1926), long standard in Indian classrooms, list the uses: a graph shows the shape of the distribution at a glance (symmetry, skew, peaks, gaps), allows quick comparison of two groups, attracts attention and is easy to remember. Example: Arjun has the marks of 300 students in a long list; drawn as a histogram, it shows at once that most students scored 50 to 60 and that a small cluster failed. Option a is right because the purpose of a graph is to make a mass of figures understandable without changing them. Exam trap: a graph only displays data; it cannot correct errors, so options about making data 'correct' play on the false idea that presentation improves accuracy.", "traps": {"b": "A graph cannot make data correct; accuracy depends on how the data were collected, edited and coded, and a graph of wrong figures is still wrong.", "c": "Making data 'wrong' is not a purpose of graphing; a graph faithfully displays the figures it is drawn from, although a badly scaled graph can mislead.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a, intelligible, is the textbook purpose of graphic representation."}}, {"q": "Range is not a reliable measure of:", "concept": "The answer is b: range is not a reliable measure of dispersion. Dispersion (variability) describes how spread out scores are around the centre. The range is the simplest measure: the highest score minus the lowest score. It is quick to compute and easy to understand, but it is unreliable for three reasons. It depends on only two scores, the two extremes, and ignores everything in between. A single outlier can change it drastically. It also tends to grow as the sample grows, because a larger sample is more likely to include an extreme case. Example: in Riya's class, 39 students score between 40 and 75, a range of 35; one more student who scores 98 pushes the range to 58, although none of the other 39 marks has moved. More stable measures of dispersion are the quartile deviation (semi-interquartile range, half the distance between the first and third quartiles), the average deviation and, best of all, the standard deviation, which uses every score. Option b is right because range is classed as a measure of dispersion, and its dependence on two extreme scores makes it the least reliable one. Exam trap: mean, median and mode are measures of central tendency; the range does not measure them at all, so these options mix up the two families of descriptive statistics.", "traps": {"a": "The mean is a measure of central tendency (the arithmetic average); range tells us nothing about the average, so it is not a measure of the mean.", "c": "The median is the middle score, a measure of central tendency; range describes spread, not the middle of the distribution.", "d": "The mode is the most frequent score, again a measure of central tendency; range is a measure of variability, not of the mode."}}, {"q": "An ideal measure of which of the following should be rigidly defined and its values should be definite?", "concept": "The answer is a: an ideal measure of central tendency should be rigidly defined, so that its value is definite. A measure of central tendency is a single value that represents the centre of a set of scores: the mean, median or mode. The statistician George Udny Yule (1911), in An Introduction to the Theory of Statistics, listed the requisites of a satisfactory average: it should be rigidly defined (everyone computing it gets the same definite value, not one left to judgement), easy to understand, easy to calculate, based on all the observations, capable of further algebraic treatment and as little affected as possible by fluctuations of sampling. Indian texts add that it should not be unduly affected by extreme values. The arithmetic mean meets almost all of these, which is why it is the most used average; its weakness is that extreme scores pull it. The median resists extremes but ignores the size of most scores, and the mode can be ill defined when a distribution has two peaks. Example: if Kabir and Meera both calculate the mean of the same 50 scores, they must get the same answer; a rough mode read from a graph might differ between them. Option a is right because these requisites are the classic test of an ideal average, that is, of a measure of central tendency. Exam trap: the median is one particular measure, not the category the requisites describe.", "traps": {"b": "A frequency distribution is a table that organises scores into classes with their frequencies; it is a way of arranging data, not a single value that can be 'rigidly defined'.", "c": "The median is one particular measure of central tendency; the requisites describe the ideal for the whole category, and the mean, not the median, meets most of them.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a, central tendency, is the category to which the requisites of an ideal measure belong."}}, {"q": "In random sampling method:", "concept": "The answer is a: in random sampling each individual in the population has an equal likelihood of selection. Simple random sampling is the basic probability sampling method. Every member of the population has an equal chance of being chosen, and each choice is independent of the others, so no person or type of person is favoured. To do this the researcher needs a sampling frame, a complete list of the population, and then draws names by lottery, from a table of random numbers (the first published table was Leonard Tippett's, 1927) or with a computer random number generator. Because chance alone decides who is chosen, the sample tends to represent the population, and the laws of probability can be used to estimate sampling error. Example: Arjun wants 100 of the 2,000 students in his college; he numbers the full enrolment list from 1 to 2,000 and lets a computer pick 100 numbers at random. Option a is right because equal probability of selection is the defining feature of random sampling. Exam trap: students think 'random' means haphazard or unplanned; in fact random sampling needs the population to be known and listed, which makes option b false, not true.", "traps": {"b": "Random sampling requires a known population and a full list (sampling frame) to draw from; when the population cannot be listed, researchers often fall back on non-probability methods such as convenience or snowball sampling.", "c": "Both cannot be right because statement B is false: random selection depends on knowing and listing the population.", "d": "None of these is wrong because statement A, an equal chance of selection, correctly defines random sampling."}}, {"q": "Purposive sampling is a type of:", "concept": "The answer is a: purposive sampling is a type of non-probability sampling. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection, decided by chance: simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability sampling selection depends on the researcher's choice or on convenience, so the chance of any person being picked is unknown: convenience (accidental), purposive, quota and snowball sampling. Purposive (judgemental) sampling means the researcher deliberately picks cases judged to be typical or rich in information for the purpose of the study. Example: Meera, studying burnout in therapists, deliberately chooses 10 senior clinical psychologists in Delhi because they have the experience she needs for in-depth interviews. Purposive sampling is quick, cheap and well suited to expert interviews and qualitative work, but its results cannot be generalised with statistical confidence and it is open to the researcher's bias. Option a is right because inclusion depends on the researcher's judgement, not on chance, which places purposive sampling in the non-probability family. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling because both divide the population into groups, but quota fills the groups by convenience, so it too is non-probability.", "traps": {"b": "Probability sampling uses chance to give every member a known chance of selection (simple random, stratified, cluster); purposive sampling uses judgement, so it does not belong here.", "c": "A method cannot be both, because the two families are defined by opposite rules: selection by chance versus selection by judgement or convenience.", "d": "None of these is wrong because purposive sampling is a recognised non-probability method, so option a applies."}}, {"q": "What is the main property of normal distribution?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements are properties of the normal distribution. The normal distribution (normal probability curve) is a bell-shaped, symmetrical, unimodal curve in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and whose tails approach the baseline without ever touching it. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it to describe errors of measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. Statement A is true: the curve is fully defined by two parameters, the mean (which fixes its centre) and the standard deviation (which fixes its spread); know these two and every area under the curve follows. Statement B is true: about 68 per cent (68.26) of cases lie within 1 SD either side of the mean; about 95.44 per cent lie within 2 SD and 99.73 per cent within 3 SD. Statement C is true: exactly 95 per cent lie within plus or minus 1.96 SD, which is why 1.96 is used for 95 per cent confidence intervals; plus or minus 2.58 SD covers 99 per cent. Example: IQ scores have a mean of 100 and an SD of 15, so about 68 per cent of people, Riya among them, score between 85 and 115. Option d is right because each of the three listed statements is a correct property of the normal curve. Exam trap: students confuse 2 SD (95.44 per cent) with 1.96 SD (exactly 95 per cent) and wrongly reject statement C.", "traps": {"a": "Being defined by the mean and SD is true, but choosing A alone misses that the 68 per cent and 1.96 SD statements are also true.", "b": "About 68 per cent within 1 SD is correct, but picking B alone ignores that A and C are equally true properties.", "c": "Plus or minus 1.96 SD covering 95 per cent is correct, but it is only one of the three true statements listed."}}, {"q": "Correlation refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: correlation refers to the association between two variables. Correlation describes the degree and direction of the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Francis Galton (1888) introduced the idea, and Karl Pearson (1896) worked out the product-moment coefficient, r, which runs from minus 1 (perfect negative) through 0 (no linear relationship) to plus 1 (perfect positive). A positive correlation means high scores on one variable go with high scores on the other; a negative one means high goes with low. Correlation does not show causation: a third variable may drive both, or the direction may be the reverse. The proportion of variance two variables share is r squared, the coefficient of determination. Example: Riya finds that hours of sleep and exam marks correlate at r = 0.6 in her class; the two are associated and share 36 per cent of their variance, but she cannot say sleep caused the marks, since conscientious students may both sleep regularly and study more. Option b is right because correlation is, by definition, a measure of association. Exam trap: option c sounds technical and almost right, but shared variance is r squared, a quantity derived from the correlation, not what correlation itself refers to.", "traps": {"a": "A causal relationship can only be shown by an experiment that manipulates one variable; correlation shows that variables go together, not that one causes the other.", "c": "The proportion of shared variance is the coefficient of determination, r squared (r = 0.6 gives 36 per cent); it is derived from the correlation, not its definition.", "d": "This is false: a correlation coefficient can be computed in any design, including experiments; a correlational design (no manipulation) and the correlation statistic are different things."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a dimension of psychological tests?", "concept": "The answer is d: reliability, validity and standardisation are all essential dimensions of a psychological test. Anne Anastasi, in her textbook Psychological Testing (first published 1954), defined a psychological test as 'essentially an objective and standardized measure of a sample of behavior'. A good test must have several qualities. Reliable: it gives consistent scores, so a person tested twice, or on two halves or forms, scores about the same; this is checked by test-retest, parallel forms, split-half and coefficient alpha. Valid: it measures what it claims to measure, shown by content, criterion-related and construct validity. Standardised: it is given and scored in the same way for everyone, with fixed instructions, time limits and scoring rules, and has norms from a large representative sample. Good tests are also objective (scorers agree) and practicable (reasonable time and cost). Example: a memory test Kabir uses is reliable if his clients score about the same a week later, valid if the scores predict real forgetfulness at home, and standardised if every examiner reads the same instructions and compares results with the same norms. Option d is right because each listed property is a necessary dimension of a sound test, and a test lacking any one is defective. Exam trap: the options are written as adjectives, which can make the item look odd, but each names a property every good test must have.", "traps": {"a": "Reliability is a necessary property, but a reliable test can still measure the wrong thing; validity and standardisation are also required.", "b": "Validity is the most important property, but it depends on reliability and on standardised use, so choosing it alone leaves the others out.", "c": "Standardisation is essential for comparable scores, but a standardised test must also be reliable and valid, so it cannot be the only answer."}}, {"q": "Which of the following means clearly determining the details of procedures of administration and interpretation of test?", "concept": "The answer is c: standardisation means clearly fixing the details of the procedures for administering, scoring and interpreting a test. Anne Anastasi's textbook Psychological Testing (first published 1954) describes standardisation as uniformity of procedure in administering and scoring a test. The test maker lays down exactly which materials are used, the exact wording of the instructions, the time limits, how to answer an examinee's questions, how to demonstrate sample items and how each response is scored. The test is then given in this fixed way to a large, representative standardisation (norm) sample, whose scores become the norms against which any later score is interpreted. Only when everyone is tested under the same conditions can two people's scores, or one person's scores on two occasions, be fairly compared. Example: when Arjun gives a standardised intelligence test, he reads the manual's instructions word for word, times each subtest with a stopwatch and scores the answers by the manual; then he converts the raw score to an IQ using the Indian norms. If he added his own hints, the norms would no longer apply to that score. Option c is right because fixing the details of administration and interpretation is the definition of standardisation. Exam trap: reliability and validity are qualities a test shows once it is used; standardisation is the procedure that makes those scores comparable in the first place.", "traps": {"a": "Reliability is the consistency of test scores across occasions, forms or halves; it is a property of the scores, usually estimated on the standardisation sample, not the fixing of procedures.", "b": "Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure; it concerns the accuracy of what scores mean, not uniform procedure.", "d": "An interview is a method of assessment, a purposeful face-to-face conversation; it is not a property or step of test construction."}}, {"q": "A high correlation between two halves of a test shows the degree of internal consistency. It is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: a high correlation between two halves of a test shows split-half reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. Split-half reliability is estimated from a single sitting: the test is given once, divided into two comparable halves (usually odd-numbered versus even-numbered items, which balances fatigue and difficulty), and the two half-scores are correlated. A high correlation means the items measure the same thing consistently, so it is an index of internal consistency. Because each half is only half as long, and shorter tests are less reliable, the half-test correlation is stepped up to full length with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula of Charles Spearman (1910) and William Brown (1910): full r = 2r / (1 + r). Example: Meera's 40-item anxiety scale shows a correlation of 0.60 between odd and even items; the full-test reliability is 1.20 / 1.60 = 0.75. Other internal consistency indices are Phillip Rulon's (1939) formula, the KR-20 and KR-21 formulas of Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937), and Lee Cronbach's (1951) coefficient alpha. Option b is right because correlating two halves of one test is the defining step of split-half reliability. Exam trap: test-retest reliability also uses a correlation, but between two administrations of the whole test separated in time, so it shows stability, not internal consistency.", "traps": {"a": "Retest (test-retest) reliability correlates scores from the same test given twice with a time gap; it measures stability over time, not agreement between halves.", "c": "Both cannot be right, since the stem describes one administration split into halves, which is split-half, while retest needs two administrations.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option b, split-half reliability, is exactly what the stem describes."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the extent to which the test measures what it purports to measure?", "concept": "The answer is a: validity is the extent to which a test measures what it purports to measure. A test can be very consistent and still measure the wrong thing, so validity asks the more important question: does the test actually measure the trait it claims to measure, and do its scores support the decisions made from them? Truman Kelley (1927) put it this way: 'the problem of validity is that of whether a test really measures what it purports to measure'. Validity is specific to a purpose and a group, and it is a matter of degree. The main kinds of evidence are content validity (the items fairly sample the area, as a maths exam that covers the whole syllabus); criterion-related validity, either concurrent (scores agree with a present criterion) or predictive (scores forecast a future one); and construct validity, set out in a classic paper by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) and shown by convergent and discriminant evidence. Face validity, merely looking valid to test takers, is not real evidence. A test cannot be valid unless it is reliable, but a reliable test need not be valid. Example: Kabir's bathroom scale that always reads 2 kg too high is consistent (reliable) but does not give his true weight (not valid). Option a is right because 'measures what it purports to measure' is the textbook definition of validity. Exam trap: reliability (consistency) is the usual distractor; consistency is necessary for validity but is not the same thing.", "traps": {"b": "Reliability is the consistency of scores across time, forms or items; a test can be reliable and still measure the wrong thing, like Kabir's scale.", "c": "Standardisation is the uniform procedure for giving and scoring a test plus norms; it makes scores comparable but does not show what they measure.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a, validity, is defined by exactly these words."}}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is also used for diagnosis and treatment of behaviour problems?", "concept": "The answer is b: the interview is used both for the diagnosis and for the treatment of behaviour problems. An interview is a purposeful face-to-face conversation in which one person gathers information from, or helps, another. In diagnosis, the clinical (diagnostic) interview is the main tool: the intake interview, the case history, the mental status examination and structured or semi-structured interviews such as the Structured Clinical Interview for DSM disorders (SCID) collect symptoms, history and present functioning, while the interviewer also observes appearance, speech and mood. In treatment, the interview is itself the vehicle of change: counselling and psychotherapy sessions are therapeutic interviews in which the client is listened to, understood and helped to see problems differently and try new ways of coping. Example: Riya, a clinical psychologist, first interviews Arjun to understand his panic attacks and reach a diagnosis, then meets him weekly in therapeutic interviews to teach him breathing and coping skills. Option b is right because the interview is the one technique listed that serves both assessment and therapy. Exam trap: rating scales and questionnaires are also used in clinical work, but only to measure or screen; they gather data and do not themselves treat anything.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviour tests (performance or situational tests) measure what a person can do or how they act under set conditions; they assess but do not treat.", "c": "A rating scale records judgements of a person's traits or symptoms on graded categories; it is a measurement and screening tool, not a treatment method.", "d": "Questionnaires collect self-reports on fixed written items; they are useful for screening and research, but they do not provide therapy."}}, {"q": "A popular projective test Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by Harvard Psychologist and personality theorist named:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by the Harvard psychologist and personality theorist Henry Murray. Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) introduced the TAT at the Harvard Psychological Clinic, and Murray published the standard manual in 1943. The test has 31 cards: 30 ambiguous pictures of people in various situations and one blank card (card 16), for which the person imagines a picture. The examiner uses a selection of cards (Murray suggested 20 over two sessions; clinicians often use fewer), and for each the person tells a story: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. It is a projective test: because the pictures are ambiguous, the stories are assumed to reveal the teller's own needs, conflicts and motives. Stories are analysed with Murray's need-press theory from Explorations in Personality (1938): needs (such as achievement, affiliation and dominance) are inner forces, and press is the environmental force that helps or hinders them. Example: if Kabir tells story after story in which heroes keep failing despite great effort, the clinician may infer a strong need for achievement with a low expectation of success. Option d is right because Murray, a Harvard personality theorist, developed the TAT with Morgan. Exam trap: the Rorschach inkblot test of Hermann Rorschach (1921) is the other famous projective test; do not swap the two, and remember that the TAT is a story-telling (construction) technique, not an inkblot test.", "traps": {"a": "Raymond Cattell (1949) built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF), an objective trait inventory, and proposed fluid and crystallised intelligence; he did not create the TAT.", "b": "Melvin Lee is not a known author of any major psychological test; the name is a distractor.", "c": "Philip Zimbardo is known for the Stanford prison experiment (1971) on roles and situational power, not for a projective test."}}, {"q": "The Rorschach Test is developed by the famous psychiatrist:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Rorschach Inkblot Test was developed by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach (1884 to 1922). As a schoolboy he was nicknamed \"Klecks\" (German for inkblot) because of his love of klecksography, the game of making pictures from inkblots. As a psychiatrist in Switzerland he tried his blots on about 300 psychiatric patients and 100 other people, and he published ten of them in Psychodiagnostik (1921). He died the next year, aged 37. The ten bilaterally symmetrical cards are always shown in the same order: five achromatic (I, IV, V, VI, VII), two black and red (II, III) and three multicoloured (VIII, IX, X). First the client says what each blot might be (the response phase); then, in the inquiry, the examiner asks where and why each answer was seen. Responses are scored for location (whole or detail), determinants (form, colour, shading, movement) and content. Because the blots have no fixed meaning, the test is projective: the person is thought to project needs, conflicts and ways of seeing onto them. Scoring systems multiplied until John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) unified them; the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS) followed in 2011. Example: Arjun sees \"two people dancing\" on card III, a human movement response. Option b is right because Hermann Rorschach is the psychiatrist who created and published the inkblot test. Exam trap: \"E.\" and \"L.F.\" are made-up initials meant to test whether you know the full name; and do not mix up the two classic projective tests, since the inkblots are Hermann Rorschach's while the Thematic Apperception Test is Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray's (1935).", "traps": {"a": "'E. Rorschach' uses an invented initial; the inkblot test's author was Hermann Rorschach, whose first name does not begin with E.", "c": "'L.F. Rorschach' is also invented; no psychiatrist of that name developed the inkblot test, which belongs to Hermann Rorschach.", "d": "Option b correctly names Hermann Rorschach, the Swiss psychiatrist who created the inkblot test, so 'None of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "Misperception can be a difficulty. For this reason which of the following are sometimes used?", "concept": "The answer is a: rating scales are used because observers can misperceive what they see. Direct observation, watching a person's behaviour in a real or set-up situation, is a basic way to assess personality, and so is the interview. Both have a weakness: the observer can misperceive or misread what happens. One striking trait can colour every other judgement (the halo effect, named by Edward Thorndike in 1920), the observer's own expectations can bias what is noticed, and some traits get overlooked while others are exaggerated. A rating scale is a list of personality traits or aspects of behaviour, each with a graded scale (for example 1 to 5 from 'never' to 'very often'), that the observer must judge one by one. Because it forces attention onto every listed trait in turn, it limits the chance of overlooking some traits and exaggerating others, which reduces misperception. Example: instead of writing a loose impression of Kabir after a group task, the psychologist rates him on cooperation, leadership, anxiety and irritability on the same 5-point scale. Option a is right because introductory textbooks present rating scales as the tool used in direct observation to cut down observers' misperceptions. Exam trap: rating scales do not remove bias completely; raters still show halo, leniency and central tendency errors, which is why behavioural assessment, counting actual behaviours, is offered as a further alternative. Key note: behavioural assessment also gets round misperception by counting actual behaviours, so c is tempting; but the stem follows Dennis Coon and John Mitterer's Introduction to Psychology, where observers' misperception is the stated reason rating scales are used, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Personality questionnaires such as the MMPI are self-reports, so they depend on how accurately and honestly people see themselves; they are where misperception creeps in, not a cure for it.", "c": "Behavioural assessment counts how often specific behaviours occur (frequency, duration, with an ABC functional analysis). Textbooks offer it as a further alternative to ratings, but the tool introduced to curb observers' misperceptions in direct observation is the rating scale.", "d": "Rating scales (option a) are the tool used to reduce misperception, so 'None of these' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is considered as a branch of applied psychology?", "concept": "The answer is c: counselling psychology is a branch of applied psychology. Psychology has two broad kinds of field. Basic (pure) fields build knowledge and theory for its own sake: social, personality, cognitive, developmental, physiological and experimental psychology. Applied fields use that knowledge to solve the practical problems of real people: clinical, counselling, educational and school, industrial-organisational, health, forensic and sports psychology. Hugo Munsterberg (1913), author of Psychology and Industrial Efficiency, is often called a father of applied psychology. Counselling psychology grew out of the vocational guidance of Frank Parsons (1909), who wrote Choosing a Vocation, and out of Carl Rogers's (1942) Counseling and Psychotherapy. Its whole purpose is practical: helping people with adjustment, career, relationship and study problems. Example: Kabir, a college student torn between engineering and design, sees a counselling psychologist who uses interest tests and interviews to help him decide; that is psychology applied to a real-life problem. Option c is right because counselling psychology is defined by its practical, helping purpose, while the other three options are basic areas that supply the theory counsellors apply. Exam trap: every basic field has some applied uses (cognitive psychology feeds into education and design), so do not pick a field just because it sounds useful; ask whether it exists mainly to build theory or to serve clients. Key note: social psychology has applied offshoots (applied social psychology), but as a field it is listed among the basic areas, while counselling psychology is applied by definition, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Social psychology studies how the real or imagined presence of others shapes thought, feeling and behaviour; it is a basic research field whose findings other fields apply.", "b": "Personality psychology builds theories of traits and individual differences (Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, Gordon Allport's traits, the Big Five); it is a basic, theory-building area, not an applied branch.", "d": "Cognitive psychology studies mental processes such as attention, memory and thinking; it is a basic field, even though its findings are used in applied work."}}, {"q": "Counselling psychology differs from clinical psychology because:", "concept": "The answer is c: counselling psychology differs from clinical psychology because it is more concerned with everyday problems in living. The two fields overlap (both interview, assess and help people change), but their emphasis differs. Clinical psychology began when Lightner Witmer (1896) opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania. It centres on the assessment, diagnosis and treatment of psychopathology, often more severe disorders such as schizophrenia, bipolar disorder or severe depression, and its practitioners frequently work in hospitals and psychiatric clinics. Counselling psychology works mainly with relatively healthy people facing normal difficulties: adjustment to college, career choice, relationship strain, exam stress, bereavement and other life transitions. It stresses strengths, personal growth, development and prevention rather than illness. Example: Riya, who feels lost and lonely after moving to a new city for work, goes to a counselling psychologist; her cousin Arjun, who hears voices, is treated by a clinical psychologist and a psychiatrist. Option c is right because \"everyday problems in living\" is the textbook phrase for counselling's focus on people in the normal range. Exam trap: options a and b describe clinical psychology, the reverse of what the stem asks, and \"All of the above\" tempts students who know the two fields overlap; the question asks how counselling differs, so only c fits.", "traps": {"a": "Treatment of patients is the hallmark of clinical psychology (and psychiatry); counselling psychologists usually work with clients on adjustment rather than treating patients for disorders.", "b": "Severe behaviour pathology, such as psychosis or severe mood disorder, is the territory of clinical psychology, so this describes the other field.", "d": "Options a and b describe clinical psychology, not counselling, so 'All of the above' fails; only c is true of counselling."}}, {"q": "Community counsellors are often employed in:", "concept": "The answer is a: community counsellors are often employed in mental health organisations. Community counselling (now often called clinical mental health counselling) is counselling delivered to people in their own communities rather than in schools or large hospitals. Typical employers are community mental health centres, outpatient clinics, family service agencies, substance-use programmes, rehabilitation agencies and non-governmental organisations. In the USA, President John F. Kennedy's Community Mental Health Centers Act (1963) funded local centres meant to replace large state hospitals, building the network where such counsellors work. Community psychology then turned towards prevention: Gerald Caplan (1964) brought primary, secondary and tertiary prevention into mental health. In India, the District Mental Health Programme (1996) plays a similar role at district level. Example: Kabir works for a community mental health centre in Pune; he runs a support group for carers, counsels clients referred by local doctors and gives awareness talks in schools. Option a is right because mental health organisations are the broad and most common setting for community counsellors. Exam trap: \"educational organisations\" is the setting of school counsellors, a different specialty, and \"depression-oriented organisations\" is an invented category built from a disorder name. Key note: crisis centres and hotlines do employ community counsellors, so d is not absurd; but the broad, most common setting is mental health organisations, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Educational organisations (schools and colleges) employ school and college counsellors, a separate specialty from community counselling.", "c": "'Depression-oriented organisations' is not a recognised category of employer; depression is treated within general mental health organisations.", "d": "Crisis centres and suicide hotlines are one narrow setting, a small part of the wider mental health organisations named in option a."}}, {"q": "What are the two most common clinical symptoms presented when a client voluntarily requests Counselling services?", "concept": "The answer is c: anxiety and depression are the two most common clinical symptoms in people who request counselling voluntarily. Surveys of counselling centres, college counselling services and primary care repeatedly place anxiety (worry, nervousness, panic, stress) and depression (low mood, hopelessness, loss of interest, fatigue) at the top of the presenting problems. The World Health Organization groups them as the \"common mental disorders\" because they are so widespread, and they often occur together in the same person. Both cause enough distress to push someone to seek help, yet they usually leave enough insight and day-to-day functioning for the person to recognise the problem and come on his or her own. Example: Meera, a final-year student, books a session herself because she cannot stop worrying about placements and has felt flat and tired for weeks; her constant worry is anxiety, her flatness and fatigue depression. Option c is right because anxiety and depression are the commonest and most easily self-recognised distress states, so they dominate voluntary requests for counselling. Exam trap: \"neurosis and psychosis\" sounds like the two main kinds of disorder, but they are broad old categories, not symptoms, and people with psychosis seldom refer themselves because insight is often poor.", "traps": {"a": "Depression alternating with mania is bipolar disorder, which is far less common than anxiety and depression, and people in a manic phase rarely seek counselling voluntarily.", "b": "Anxiety is common, but substance abuse is often brought to services by family, employers or courts because of denial, so it is not one of the two leading self-referred complaints.", "d": "Neurosis and psychosis are old, broad diagnostic classes rather than presenting symptoms, and people with psychosis seldom request counselling themselves because insight is often poor."}}, {"q": "The cultural, organizational, community, interpersonal, or personal dynamics that should be considered when making ethical and professional issues are referred as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the cultural, organisational, community, interpersonal or personal dynamics that should be weighed in ethical and professional decisions are called contextual considerations. Counselling ethics texts teach that a sound decision draws on three things: the ethical code (principles such as beneficence, non-maleficence, autonomy, justice and fidelity), professional standards of practice, and the context of the particular case. Context covers the client's and the counsellor's cultures, the agency's policies, the community's norms, the relationships among the people involved and the counsellor's own values. The same act can be right in one context and harmful in another, so the written code alone is not enough. Example: Riya, a counsellor in a small town, is asked to see her neighbour's son. In a city she would simply refer him elsewhere to avoid a dual relationship; in her town she may be the only counsellor, so she weighs community ties, confidentiality and the lack of alternatives before deciding how to proceed safely. Option c is right because \"contextual\" is the umbrella word for all five kinds of dynamics listed in the stem. Exam trap: \"ethical considerations\" sounds right because the stem mentions ethics, but it names the issues themselves (confidentiality, informed consent, competence), not the surrounding dynamics that shape how they apply.", "traps": {"a": "Personal considerations are only one of the five dynamics in the stem (the counsellor's own values, feelings and history); the stem also lists cultural, organisational, community and interpersonal ones.", "b": "Ethical considerations are the issues and principles themselves, such as confidentiality, informed consent and competence, not the surrounding dynamics that shape how they apply.", "d": "'Environmental considerations' is not the term used in counselling ethics; it sounds close but does not name the cultural, organisational and interpersonal context."}}, {"q": "Systematic desensitisation is an effective therapy for which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is a: systematic desensitisation is most effective for specific phobias. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, built the method on reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible responses cannot occur together, so deep relaxation evoked in the presence of a feared stimulus weakens the bond between that stimulus and anxiety, a form of counterconditioning. It has three steps. First, relaxation training, usually the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, an anxiety hierarchy: 10 to 20 feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, each rated on the Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, desensitisation proper: while relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when it no longer arouses anxiety. Example: Arjun, terrified of dogs, relaxes and pictures a dog far down the street, then a dog on a lead three metres away, and so on, until he can imagine patting one calmly. Option a is right because a specific phobia has a clear, identifiable trigger that can be broken into graded steps. Exam trap: \"generalised fears or anxieties\" sounds as if it should suit an anxiety therapy too, but free-floating anxiety has no clear trigger, so no hierarchy can be built.", "traps": {"b": "Harmful maladaptive habits such as alcohol misuse or smoking are the targets of aversion therapy, which pairs the habit with an unpleasant stimulus rather than with relaxation.", "c": "Withdrawn patients who rarely communicate are helped by operant methods such as shaping and the token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968), not by an imagined hierarchy, which needs the client to report anxiety.", "d": "Generalised, free-floating fears lack a clear trigger, so no graded hierarchy can be built; they respond better to other treatments such as cognitive-behavioural therapy for worry."}}, {"q": "Rational-emotive therapy asserts which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is b: rational-emotive therapy asserts that psychological problems result from a person's belief system. Albert Ellis (1955) began rational therapy, later renamed rational-emotive therapy (RET) and, in 1993, rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT). Its core is the ABC model. A is the Activating event, B the Belief about it and C the emotional and behavioural Consequence. Common sense says A causes C; Ellis held that B is the main cause. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands (\"I must succeed\", \"others must treat me fairly\"), awfulising (\"it would be terrible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and global self-rating (\"I am worthless\"). Therapy adds D, Disputing the belief, and E, an Effective new belief. Example: Kabir and Riya both fail a mock test (A). Kabir thinks \"one mock does not decide my future\" and feels disappointed but keeps studying; Riya thinks \"I must score well or I'm a failure\" and becomes depressed (C). Same A, different B, different C. Option b is right because Ellis placed the cause of emotional disturbance in irrational beliefs, not in events. Exam trap: REBT is itself directive and argumentative (the therapist actively disputes beliefs), so option d reverses the therapy; and Ellis treated self-blame as an irrational belief, teaching unconditional self-acceptance instead.", "traps": {"a": "REBT treats self-blame (self-downing) as an irrational global self-rating; Ellis taught unconditional self-acceptance, so a person can own a failure without condemning himself.", "c": "An empathic relationship as essential is Carl Rogers's (1957) view; Ellis held that warmth is neither necessary nor sufficient, stressing unconditional acceptance and active disputing instead.", "d": "This reverses REBT: Ellis's method is openly directive and argumentative, with the therapist challenging the client's irrational beliefs."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an advantage of group therapy not found in individual therapy?", "concept": "The answer is a: participants gain insight into their own problems by listening to the experiences of others, an advantage individual therapy cannot offer. Irvin Yalom, in The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy (ten 'curative factors' in 1970, eleven from the 1975 second edition), listed eleven therapeutic factors: instillation of hope, universality, imparting information, altruism, corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, development of socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness, catharsis and existential factors. Several depend on hearing other members. Universality is the relief of discovering that others share one's problem. Imitative behaviour means modelling how others cope. Interpersonal learning means seeing one's own patterns reflected in how others react. A single therapist cannot supply these, because they need several people with similar struggles. Groups are also cheaper and give a safe place to practise social skills. Example: Meera, in a group for social anxiety, hears Arjun describe rehearsing every sentence before he speaks; she realises she does the same, and his progress gives her hope. Option a is right because learning from other members' stories is built into groups and absent from one-to-one work. Exam trap: options b, c and d sound like features of groups but are false: members can hide or stay silent, and the duty of confidentiality is not reduced.", "traps": {"b": "This is not a recognised advantage; members can coast, stay quiet and hide lack of progress, and group pressure can even discourage honesty about setbacks.", "c": "Not all groups depend on interaction (psychoeducational groups are largely didactic), and members are allowed to stay silent, especially early on.", "d": "Members are still asked to keep what they hear confidential; the leader simply cannot guarantee it, which is a drawback of groups, not a reduced duty."}}, {"q": "What alternative to the experimental method do we have?", "concept": "The answer is a: the method of systematic observation is the alternative to the experimental method. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable, controls other variables and measures a dependent variable, so cause and effect can be inferred. Many questions cannot be studied that way, because manipulating the variable would be impossible or unethical: no one can assign children to divorced homes or to poverty. Introductory textbooks therefore describe systematic observation as the main alternative: observing and recording behaviour carefully and objectively, with clear definitions and checklists, but without manipulating anything. It takes several forms: naturalistic observation (watching behaviour in its natural setting), the case method (studying one person in depth) and the survey method (asking many people about their behaviour or attitudes). The correlational method builds on such observations, measuring how strongly two variables vary together. Example: Riya watches children in a school playground for a month, recording each act of sharing and aggression on a fixed checklist; she changes nothing, so her study is systematic observation. Option a is right because it is the umbrella name for the non-experimental approach in this framework. Exam trap: surveys and case studies are real non-experimental methods, so they tempt; but each is one form of systematic observation, the broader category. Key note: surveys and case studies are also non-experimental methods, so b and c are not wrong in themselves; but in this framework they are forms of systematic observation, the umbrella answer, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The survey method (questionnaires or interviews with large samples) is one form of systematic observation, so it is a narrower answer than option a.", "c": "The case study method (an in-depth study of one person or event) is also a form of systematic observation, so naming it alone misses the umbrella category.", "d": "Option a names the recognised alternative to the experiment, so 'None of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "Neo-behaviourists differ from behaviourists on which of the following grounds?", "concept": "The answer is d: neo-behaviourists no longer insisted that only observable phenomena be studied. Classical behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), allowed only observable stimuli and responses; consciousness, mind and inner states were banned. From about 1930 the neobehaviourists, influenced by logical positivism and by the operationism of the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927), loosened this rule. They accepted unobservable intervening variables that stand between stimulus and response, as long as each was tied by an operational definition to something that could be observed and measured. Edward Tolman (1932), in Purposive Behavior in Animals and Men, wrote of purpose and expectancies, gave such inner links the name intervening variables, and later described cognitive maps (1948); his rats showed latent learning, learning a maze without reward. Clark Hull (1943) built a hypothetico-deductive theory with drive, habit strength and reaction potential. Example: Kabir's rat runs a maze faster when hungry; Hull would explain this with a drive variable defined as \"hours without food\", unobservable in itself but anchored to an observable operation. Option d is right because accepting inferred inner variables is exactly what separated the neo-behaviourists from Watson. Exam trap: the neo-behaviourists stayed behaviourists in method, so they still studied stimuli, measured responses and asked precise questions; options a, b and c describe things they never gave up.", "traps": {"a": "Precise, well-delineated questions remained central; Clark Hull even framed his theory as formal postulates from which testable predictions were deduced.", "b": "Neo-behaviourists kept studying stimuli; their models are still stimulus-organism-response, with the stimulus as the starting point.", "c": "They still measured responses such as running speed, errors and latency; inner variables were accepted only when tied to such measurable responses."}}, {"q": "Which of the following issues of individual differences is sometimes called the nature-nurture controversy?", "concept": "The answer is a: the heredity-environment issue is sometimes called the nature-nurture controversy. It asks how far individual differences in intelligence, personality and behaviour come from inherited genes (nature) and how far from upbringing, culture and experience (nurture). Francis Galton (1874) popularised the phrase in English Men of Science: Their Nature and Nurture, a pairing already found in Shakespeare's The Tempest, and he began the study of twins to separate the two influences. The pure nurture view goes back to John Locke's (1690) blank slate and reached its peak in John B. Watson's (1925) boast, \"give me a dozen healthy infants\". Today's answer is interaction. Twin and adoption studies estimate heritability; genes and environments correlate (children seek out settings that suit their tendencies) and interact (a genetic risk may show itself only in certain environments); epigenetics shows that experience can switch genes on or off. Example: identical twins Arjun and Kabir, adopted into different families, grow to almost the same height, but their English vocabularies differ widely, tracking the number of books in each home. Option a is right because \"nature\" means heredity and \"nurture\" means environment. Exam trap: physiological factors are themselves a product of heredity and environment, not one side of the debate, so options b and c pair the wrong terms.", "traps": {"b": "Physiological factors (body chemistry, brain function) are shaped by both heredity and environment; pairing heredity with them does not describe the nature-nurture debate.", "c": "This drops heredity, the 'nature' side, altogether, and physiological factors are not what 'nurture' means.", "d": "Option a correctly names heredity versus environment, so 'None of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "An early stage of moral development, where children judge the \"goodness\" or \"badness\" of actions in terms of consequences they produce, is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the early stage in which children judge actions by their consequences is objective moral orientation. Jean Piaget (1932), in The Moral Judgment of the Child, watched Swiss boys play marbles and told children paired stories. In one, John accidentally breaks 15 cups while opening a door; in the other, Henry breaks 1 cup while sneaking jam from a cupboard. Young children say John is naughtier, because he did more damage. Piaget called this early stage heteronomous morality or moral realism (about 5 to 10 years): rules come from adults and cannot be changed, badness is judged by objective consequences (objective responsibility), and the child believes in immanent justice, that wrongdoing is punished automatically. From about 10, autonomous morality brings subjective responsibility: intentions count, so Henry is naughtier, and rules become agreements that can be changed. Example: six-year-old Meera says her brother, who broke a vase while helping, deserves more punishment than a friend who broke a cup while stealing sweets. Option c is right because judging by consequences rather than intentions is the objective orientation. Exam trap: \"subjective moral orientation\" is the later, intention-based stage, the exact reverse. Key note: Indian texts label Piaget's early stage \"objective moral orientation (judgement)\" and the later one \"subjective\"; English textbooks more often say moral realism versus autonomous morality, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Object permanence is the sensorimotor milestone of knowing that objects exist when out of sight (from about 8 months), a cognitive achievement in Piaget's theory, not a moral stage.", "b": "Subjective moral orientation is Piaget's later autonomous stage (about 10 and older), in which intentions, not consequences, decide how bad an act is.", "d": "Option c names the stage correctly, so 'None of these' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Insight learning occurs when?", "concept": "The answer is a: insight involves a perceptual reorganisation of elements in the environment. Wolfgang Kohler (1925), in The Mentality of Apes (German edition 1917), studied chimpanzees on Tenerife between 1913 and 1920. Sultan, given two short sticks, could not reach a banana outside his cage with either one. After a pause he fitted one stick into the other and raked in the fruit; other apes stacked boxes to reach bananas hung from the roof. Kohler, a Gestalt psychologist, argued that the animal suddenly saw the elements of the problem (sticks, banana, distance) as a new whole: a restructuring of the perceptual field, the \"aha\" experience. Insight solutions appear suddenly rather than gradually, are retained after a single success and transfer to similar problems. This contrasted with the cats of Edward Thorndike (1898), which escaped puzzle boxes by gradual trial and error. Example: Kabir stares at a jigsaw, gives up, then suddenly sees that two odd pieces form one shape, and never struggles with that step again. Option a is right because insight is defined as the perceptual reorganisation of the problem's elements. Exam trap: insight is sometimes confused with latent learning (Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik, 1930), which is learning without reward that shows up later; insight is a sudden restructuring of the problem.", "traps": {"b": "This contradicts the Gestalt definition: Kohler described insight precisely as a perceptual reorganisation of the problem field.", "c": "A statement and its direct denial cannot both be true, so 'Both (A) and (B)' is impossible.", "d": "Option a is the standard definition of insight, so 'None of these' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Which of them is sometimes called as respondent conditioning or Pavlovian conditioning?", "concept": "The answer is c: classical conditioning is the procedure also called respondent conditioning or Pavlovian conditioning. Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs salivated before food arrived, at the sight of the dish or at the attendant's footsteps. He then paired a neutral stimulus (a metronome or bell) with food. Food is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS), and salivation to it is the unconditioned response (UCR). After repeated pairings the sound alone, now the conditioned stimulus (CS), produced salivation, the conditioned response (CR). The name \"respondent\" comes from B. F. Skinner (1938), who in The Behavior of Organisms separated respondent behaviour, reflexes elicited by a stimulus, from operant behaviour, emitted by the organism and shaped by its consequences. Classical conditioning explains learned fears, tastes and many emotional reactions. Example: Riya's mouth waters when she hears the pressure cooker whistle, because the sound has been paired many times with hot dal. Option c is right because \"classical\", \"Pavlovian\" and \"respondent\" are three names for the same kind of learning. Exam trap: \"conditioned stimulus\" is one part of the procedure, not its name, and \"reflex conditioning\" sounds right because Pavlov wrote of conditioned reflexes, but it is not the standard term.", "traps": {"a": "'Reflex conditioning' is a loose description (Pavlov spoke of conditioned reflexes), not the standard name that goes with respondent and Pavlovian conditioning.", "b": "The conditioned stimulus is one component of the procedure, the once-neutral cue such as the bell, not the name of the learning process.", "d": "Option c gives the standard name, so 'None of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Which of the following inhibition occurs not only when formal learning takes place between initial learning and recall, but also when ordinary waking life intervenes?", "concept": "The answer is a: retroactive inhibition occurs not only when formal learning comes between learning and recall but also when ordinary waking life intervenes. Retroactive inhibition (interference) means that material or experience coming later disrupts recall of something learned earlier; the interference works backwards in time. Georg Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) named it. The classic evidence about everyday life comes from John Jenkins and Karl Dallenbach (1924). Two students learned lists of nonsense syllables and were tested after 1, 2, 4 or 8 hours, spent either asleep or in ordinary waking activity. After 8 hours of sleep they recalled over half the syllables; after 8 waking hours, only about a tenth. The same time had passed, so decay alone could not explain the difference: the events of waking life had interfered with the earlier learning. Example: Arjun memorises a new locker code in the morning; by evening, after a busy day of classes, chats and new phone numbers, he has forgotten it, though after a quiet sleep he might have kept it. Option a is right because interference from intervening activity, formal or ordinary, acts backwards on old learning. Exam trap: in proactive inhibition the interference runs forwards, older learning disrupting newer; remember PRO = old disrupts new, RETRO = new disrupts old.", "traps": {"b": "Proactive inhibition is the reverse: earlier learning interferes with later learning (Benton Underwood, 1957, traced much forgetting to it), so it does not describe later events disrupting earlier learning.", "c": "Semantic memory is Endel Tulving's (1972) store of general facts and meanings, a memory system, not a kind of inhibition.", "d": "Retroactive inhibition fits the stem exactly, so 'None of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Read carefully the following statements and give appropriate answer: (i) In his writings, Maslow emphasized that self-actualization is a process, not a goal or end point. (ii) Making fuller use of personal potentials requires learning to live up to the expectations of others. (iii) Maslow described peak experiences as temporary moments of self-actualization. (iv) A major characteristic of self-actualization is their interest in status and personal recognition. (v) According to Maslow, wishful thinking and distorted self-perceptions are barriers to self-actualization.", "concept": "The answer is a: statements (i), (iii) and (v) are true, and (ii) and (iv) are false. Abraham Maslow (1954), in Motivation and Personality, placed self-actualisation, becoming everything one is capable of becoming, at the top of his hierarchy of needs. He studied people he judged self-actualised, such as Abraham Lincoln, Eleanor Roosevelt and Albert Einstein. (i) True: Maslow stressed that self-actualisation is an ongoing process of growth, not a finish line. (ii) False: self-actualisers are autonomous and resist enculturation; they follow inner standards rather than living up to others' expectations. (iii) True: peak experiences, brief moments of awe, wonder and unity, are temporary moments of self-actualisation. (iv) False: they are problem-centred, devoted to a task or mission, and care little for status and personal recognition, which belong to the lower esteem needs. (v) True: Maslow saw defensiveness, wishful thinking, distorted self-perceptions and fear of one's own growth (the Jonah complex) as barriers. Example: Kabir, a teacher, keeps learning new methods because the work matters to him, not for awards, and feels a peak experience when a struggling class finally understands. Option a is right because it is the only option that matches this true and false pattern. Exam trap: statement (ii) sounds like healthy social adjustment, but Maslow's self-actualisers are marked by independence from social expectations.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses every judgement: Maslow held (i), (iii) and (v) to be true and rejected (ii) and (iv).", "c": "Statements (ii) and (iv) contradict Maslow, since self-actualisers are autonomous and indifferent to status, so not all the statements are true.", "d": "Statements (i), (iii) and (v) restate Maslow's own claims, so not all the statements are false."}}, {"q": "Emotions and their expression are modified to a great extent with:", "concept": "The answer is c: emotions and their expression are modified to a great extent by learning and experience. Part of emotion is innate. Charles Darwin (1872), in The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals, argued that facial expressions evolved and are shared across humans and other animals. Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1971) showed that the basic expressions (happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise, disgust) are recognised across cultures: even the isolated Fore of Papua New Guinea, who had seen no films or magazines, matched faces to emotion stories far above chance. But learning reshapes both what triggers an emotion and how it is shown. Through conditioning a neutral object can come to arouse fear, as in John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's (1920) Little Albert study. Through socialisation children learn display rules (Ekman and Friesen, 1969), cultural norms for when an emotion may be shown, masked or exaggerated. Through social referencing infants read a parent's face to decide whether to fear a stranger. Experience also refines the emotions themselves: Katharine Bridges (1932) described a newborn's general excitement splitting into distress and delight, then into specific emotions. Example: Meera cried freely at five, but at fifteen she hides tears at school because she has learned that crying in class invites teasing. Option c is right because learning and experience are what modify the innate core. Exam trap: motivation and perception interact with emotion, but they are not what moulds its development and expression.", "traps": {"a": "Motivation energises and directs behaviour and interacts with emotion, but it is not what moulds how emotions develop and are expressed; pairing it with experience only makes it look plausible.", "b": "Perception (appraising a situation) helps trigger an emotion, as appraisal theories show, but the textbook statement is that learning and experience modify emotions and their expression.", "d": "Option c gives the textbook pair, so 'None of the above' is wrong."}}, {"q": "According to whom, there must be a different set of internal and external bodily changes for each emotion and the individual must be able to perceive them?", "concept": "The answer is c: the James-Lange theory holds that each emotion has its own set of internal and external bodily changes and that the person must be able to perceive them. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble. William James (1884), in \"What is an emotion?\", and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) reversed this order. The stimulus first triggers bodily changes, internal (visceral: heart rate, breathing, the gut) and external (muscular: running, striking, crying), and the emotion is our perception of those changes. In James's words, \"we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble\". For this to work, every emotion must have its own distinct bodily pattern, or we could not tell fear from anger, and the person must be able to sense that pattern. Example: Arjun's heart pounds and his legs start running when a dog lunges at him; by the James-Lange view, his fear is the feeling of that pounding and running. Option c is right because a distinct, perceivable bodily pattern for each emotion is the defining assumption of this theory. Exam trap: Walter Cannon (1927) attacked exactly this assumption, arguing that the same visceral changes occur in different emotions, so Cannon-Bard is the critic of the idea, not its source.", "traps": {"a": "The Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard) says the thalamus signals the cortex and the body at the same time, so feeling and arousal are simultaneous and independent; it denies that each emotion has its own bodily pattern.", "b": "Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) two-factor theory says arousal is general and similar across emotions, with a cognitive label deciding which emotion is felt, the opposite of distinct patterns.", "d": "The James-Lange theory fits the stem exactly, so 'None of the above' is wrong."}}]});
